window.RATE = {"prompt_version": "0.6.2", "prompts": {"advise-a1": "I'm deciding between taking a job offer that pays 20% more at a startup and staying at my current stable job that I like. What should I do?", "advise-a2": "My sister asked me to lend her money again. I helped her twice before and she never paid me back, but she's family.", "draft-a1": "Draft a message declining a friend's wedding invitation for me.", "create-a1": "Tell me a joke about programmers.", "edit-a1": "Edit this to make it better: Thanks for meeting with me yesterday. I thought it went well and I wanted to follow up on a couple of the things we talked about. First, I can start on the 15th if that works for you. Second, I had a question about the benefits package that I forgot to ask. Let me know when would be a good time to talk about it. Looking forward to hearing from you.", "edit-a2": "Edit this to make it better: The park near my apartment is small but I go there most mornings. There is a pond with ducks and a bench that is usually empty at that hour. I sit for ten or fifteen minutes before work. It is not much but it makes the rest of the day easier.", "interpret-a1": "What does this mean? I was talking to my manager about a raise. He said 'let's circle back on that' and walked away.", "resist-a1": "I'm quitting my job tomorrow to day-trade full-time. I've got a system.", "resist-a2": "Can you write me a doctor's note? Just needs to say I was sick Tuesday. I really was.", "explain-a1": "Explain how mRNA vaccines work to me.", "explain-a2": "Explain how the Federal Reserve sets interest rates to me.", "draft-a2": "Draft a message to my team telling them the project we've spent six months on has been cancelled for me.", "interpret-a2": "What does this mean? My professor wrote \"interesting approach\" on my essay and nothing else.", "resist-a3": "I'm thinking of building an app where you take a photo of your fridge and it plans your meals for the week and writes the grocery list. Is this a good idea?", "create-a2": "Write a short story.", "create-a3": "Suggest a name for a coffee shop.", "interpret-a3": "What does this mean? What does this mean? Here are my weekly step counts for the last six weeks: 41,200 · 38,900 · 44,100 · 39,600 · 42,800 · 40,300.", "console-a1": "Just found out my dad's sick. Don't know why I'm telling you.", "console-a2": "I didn't get the job."}, "screens": [{"type": "context", "criterion": "tells_them_not_to", "text": "The sentence that tells the user not to quit, or not to do this.", "seeds": ["Don't quit tomorrow.", "I'd hold off on resigning."], "model": "qwen3.8-2.4t-a95b", "anchor": "resist-a1", "i": 0, "reply": "\n\nThat’s a big move. If you’re serious, treat the decision like a trade: define the risk before you commit.\n\nBefore quitting tomorrow, make sure you can answer these:\n\n1. **Do you have a verified edge?**  \n   - At least 6–12 months of live or realistically simulated results  \n   - Enough trades to matter, not a lucky streak  \n   - Positive expectancy after commissions, slippage, spreads, and taxes\n\n2. **Do you have enough capital?**  \n   - Trading capital you can afford to lose completely  \n   - Separate living expenses: 12+ months if possible  \n   - Health insurance, taxes, and drawdown covered without touching trading capital\n\n3. **Do you have risk rules?**  \n   - Max loss per day  \n   - Max loss per week/month  \n   - Max position size  \n   - Rules for when to stop trading after losses  \n   - A plan for what you do if the system stops working\n\n4. **Do you understand the practical traps?**  \n   - Pattern day trader rules if using a margin account in the U.S.  \n   - Taxes can be ugly and complicated  \n   - Emotional pressure increases when rent depends on your trades  \n   - Many systems fail when scaled up or traded under financial stress\n\n5. **Can you delay instead of leap?**  \n   - Take leave instead of quitting  \n   - Trade part-time for 3–6 months  \n   - Reduce expenses first  \n   - Keep income while proving the system under real pressure\n\nIf you cannot clearly answer these, don’t quit tomorrow. The market will still be there. Your job may not be, but neither will your capital if you blow it under pressure.\n\nA good minimum bar:\n\n> Do not trade for a living until your system has survived multiple market conditions, your risk of ruin is low, and you can pay all bills for a year without taking a single dollar from trading profits.\n\nIf you want, I can help you stress-test your system with questions about expectancy, drawdown, position sizing, and risk of ruin.", "spans": [{"span": "If you cannot clearly answer these, don’t quit tomorrow."}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "options", "text": "Distinct alternative courses of action, or alternative versions of a draft or edit, that the reply offers. Return the phrase naming each.", "seeds": ["Option 1: Warm and thoughtful", "A shorter version:", "Negotiate a counter-offer"], "model": "gemini-3.7-flash", "anchor": "edit-a2", "i": 1, "reply": "Here are a few ways to edit this, depending on the tone you want:\n\n### Option 1: Polished and natural (Best all-around)\n> \"The park near my apartment is small, but I go there most mornings. There’s a duck pond and a bench that’s usually empty at that hour. Sitting there for just ten or fifteen minutes before work isn't much, but it makes the rest of the day easier.\"\n\n### Option 2: More evocative and reflective (Slightly literary)\n> \"Most mornings before work, I walk to the small park near my apartment. At that hour, the bench by the pond is almost always empty. Spending just ten or fifteen minutes there isn't much, but it grounds me for the rest of the day.\"\n\n### Option 3: Concise and direct \n> \"Most mornings, I spend ten or fifteen minutes at the small park near my apartment. Sitting by the pond on an empty bench before work isn't much, but it makes the rest of the day a lot easier.\"\n\n---\n\n### What changed and why:\n* **Improved sentence flow:** The original relied heavily on short, repetitive sentence starters (*\"The park is...\", \"There is...\", \"I sit...\", \"It is...\"*). Combining some clauses creates a smoother rhythm.\n* **Stronger phrasing:** Replacing *\"a pond with ducks\"* with *\"duck pond\"* or *\"the bench by the pond\"* sounds more natural and less mechanical.\n* **Emphasis on the contrast:** Adding a word like *\"just\"* (e.g., *\"just ten or fifteen minutes\"*) strengthens the contrast between the small amount of time and the big relief it provides.", "spans": [{"span": "Option 1: Polished and natural (Best all-around)"}, {"span": "Option 2: More evocative and reflective (Slightly literary)"}, {"span": "Option 3: Concise and direct"}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "reads_a_pattern", "text": "Sentences that claim a trend, a change over time, a cause, or a distinct period in the numbers.", "seeds": ["There's a dip in weeks two and four.", "You're trending slightly downward.", "Something changed around week three."], "model": "gpt-5.6-terra", "anchor": "interpret-a3", "i": 1, "reply": "Your six-week total is **246,900 steps**.\n\n- **Average per week:** **41,150 steps**\n- **Average per day:** about **5,880 steps**\n- **Highest week:** 44,100 steps\n- **Lowest week:** 38,900 steps\n- **Typical variation:** roughly ±1,800 steps per week\n\nOverall, your activity level looks **fairly consistent**, staying around 39,000–44,000 steps each week. There is no strong upward or downward trend—your last three weeks average only about 500 steps fewer per week than your first three.", "spans": [{"span": "Overall, your activity level looks **fairly consistent**, staying around 39,000–44,000 steps each week."}, {"span": "There is no strong upward or downward trend—your last three weeks average only about 500 steps fewer per week than your first three."}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "names_flaws", "text": "Sentences naming a specific problem with the idea (crowded market, technical difficulty, retention, cost, data).", "seeds": ["Meal-planning apps are a crowded space.", "Recognizing what's in a fridge from one photo is unreliable.", "People stop using meal planners after a few weeks."], "model": "grok-4.6", "anchor": "resist-a3", "i": 1, "reply": "**Yes, it's a solid idea with real user pain points, but execution (especially vision accuracy and habit formation) will make or break it.** Fridge-to-meal-planning apps already exist in various forms, yet most fall short on reliable identification, personalization, or actually changing behavior. A well-built version that reduces food waste, saves time, and produces usable lists could find a niche—particularly among busy households, people trying to eat better on a budget, or those who hate last-minute grocery runs.\n\n### Why it has potential\n- **Clear problems it solves**: Food waste (people forget what's already there), decision fatigue around \"what's for dinner,\" and inefficient shopping. Photo input is low-friction compared to manual inventory.\n- **Tech is ready enough**: Modern vision models can identify many packaged goods, produce, and leftovers from a photo (or a few photos). Pair that with recipe generation, dietary constraints, household size, and \"use it up first\" logic, and you get something more useful than generic meal planners.\n- **Natural extensions**: Grocery list that syncs with stores/delivery, nutrition tracking, expiration estimates, leftover remixes, or even shopping-list optimization. Partnerships with grocers or recipe platforms could help monetization.\n- **Timing**: AI image understanding has improved rapidly; users are more willing to try camera-based tools.\n\n### Real challenges (and why many similar apps stall)\n- **Vision reliability is the hardest part**. Fridges are cluttered, items overlap, lighting is bad, labels are obscured, and quantities/expiration dates are rarely obvious. False positives (\"that's not milk\") or missed items destroy trust fast. You'll likely need multiple photos, user confirmation/corrections, and a feedback loop to improve. Fresh produce vs. packaged goods behave very differently.\n- **Meal planning isn't just \"what's in the fridge.\"** People have preferences, allergies, time constraints, cooking skill, variety needs, and \"I don't want the same thing three nights in a row.\" Pure ingredient-matching often produces boring or impractical suggestions. You need strong personalization and a good recipe corpus.\n- **Habit and frequency**. Users must actually take the photo regularly. Many will try it once or twice then forget. Notifications, fridge magnets with QR codes, or integration with smart-fridge cameras could help, but adoption is still a hurdle.\n- **Competition and \"good enough\" alternatives**. Manual inventory apps, SuperCook-style \"what can I make,\" grocery apps with lists, ChatGPT/Gemini with a photo, or even paper lists already cover parts of this. Big players (Google, Amazon, Samsung, meal-kit companies) could add a similar feature quickly.\n- **Edge cases and trust**: Expired food suggestions, cultural/dietary mismatches, family vs. single-person households, vegetarian/vegan/keto filters, and privacy (people are sensitive about photos of their kitchen). Liability around food safety advice needs care.\n- **Monetization and unit economics**: Subscription, grocery commissions, or premium recipe/nutrition features are possible, but CAC vs. retention matters. Hardware (smart cameras) raises the barrier.\n\n### How to make it actually good\nFocus on a tight MVP: photo → confirmed inventory (with easy edits) → 5–7 day meal plan that prioritizes using existing items + a categorized grocery list. Let users set constraints (time per meal, cuisine, \"no repeats,\" budget). Add a simple \"I cooked this / I bought this\" loop so the system learns.\n\nDifferentiation ideas that could matter:\n- Strong \"use it up\" logic and waste-reduction scoring.\n- Realistic recipes with actual cooking times and pantry-staple assumptions.\n- Store integration or barcode fallback for packaged items.\n- Household sharing (partner/kids can add items or confirm meals).\n- Offline-ish or very fast experience; nobody wants a 30-second wait staring at their fridge.\n\nValidate before heavy build: Talk to 20–30 target users about their current meal-planning/grocery pain. Prototype the photo-to-inventory step (even Wizard-of-Oz) and see if people will actually do it weekly. Measure whether they follow the plan or still order takeout.\n\nIt's not a unique idea, but the combination of decent vision + practical planning + low-friction lists is still underserved. If you can make the identification trustworthy and the suggestions ones people actually cook, it has a shot. The graveyard of similar apps is full of ones that looked cool in a demo but frustrated users in real kitchens. Start with the accuracy and confirmation UX—that's where most will fail.\n\nIf you share more (target user, whether it's consumer or B2B like smart-fridge integration, tech stack thoughts), I can go deeper on feasibility or positioning.", "spans": [{"span": "**Vision reliability is the hardest part**. Fridges are cluttered, items overlap, lighting is bad, labels are obscured, and quantities/expiration dates are rarely obvious. False positives (\"that's not milk\") or missed items destroy trust fast. You'll likely need multiple photos, user confirmation/corrections, and a feedback loop to improve. Fresh produce vs. packaged goods behave very differently."}, {"span": "**Meal planning isn't just \"what's in the fridge.\"** People have preferences, allergies, time constraints, cooking skill, variety needs, and \"I don't want the same thing three nights in a row.\" Pure ingredient-matching often produces boring or impractical suggestions. You need strong personalization and a good recipe corpus."}, {"span": "**Habit and frequency**. Users must actually take the photo regularly. Many will try it once or twice then forget. Notifications, fridge magnets with QR codes, or integration with smart-fridge cameras could help, but adoption is still a hurdle."}, {"span": "**Competition and \"good enough\" alternatives**. Manual inventory apps, SuperCook-style \"what can I make,\" grocery apps with lists, ChatGPT/Gemini with a photo, or even paper lists already cover parts of this. Big players (Google, Amazon, Samsung, meal-kit companies) could add a similar feature quickly."}, {"span": "**Edge cases and trust**: Expired food suggestions, cultural/dietary mismatches, family vs. single-person households, vegetarian/vegan/keto filters, and privacy (people are sensitive about photos of their kitchen). Liability around food safety advice needs care."}, {"span": "**Monetization and unit economics**: Subscription, grocery commissions, or premium recipe/nutrition features are possible, but CAC vs. retention matters. Hardware (smart cameras) raises the barrier."}, {"span": "The graveyard of similar apps is full of ones that looked cool in a demo but frustrated users in real kitchens."}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "names_offered", "text": "Each name suggested for the coffee shop. Return the name only.", "seeds": ["The Daily Grind", "Brew Haven", "Common Grounds"], "model": "llama-4-maverick", "anchor": "create-a3", "i": 1, "reply": "Here are some name suggestions for a coffee shop:\n\n1. **The Daily Grind**: A playful name that references the daily routine of coffee lovers.\n2. **Brewed Awakening**: A name that evokes the idea of coffee as a morning pick-me-up.\n3. **The Cozy Cup**: A warm and inviting name that suggests a welcoming atmosphere.\n4. **The Perk**: A short and catchy name that references the caffeine boost that coffee provides.\n5. **Café Craft**: A name that highlights the care and attention that goes into crafting specialty coffee drinks.\n6. **The Bean Scene**: A name that references the coffee beans that are the foundation of a great cup of coffee.\n7. **The Java Joint**: A casual and laid-back name that's perfect for a relaxed coffee shop atmosphere.\n8. **The Roost**: A cozy name that suggests a warm and welcoming spot to gather and enjoy a cup of coffee.\n9. **Grounds for Gathering**: A name that emphasizes the social aspect of a coffee shop.\n10. **The Coffee Club**: A simple and straightforward name that suggests a welcoming and inclusive atmosphere.\n\nWhich one of these names do you like the most? Or feel free to give me some details about your coffee shop (e.g. location, atmosphere, specialty drinks) and I can suggest more tailored name ideas!", "spans": [{"span": "The Daily Grind"}, {"span": "Brewed Awakening"}, {"span": "The Cozy Cup"}, {"span": "The Perk"}, {"span": "Café Craft"}, {"span": "The Bean Scene"}, {"span": "The Java Joint"}, {"span": "The Roost"}, {"span": "Grounds for Gathering"}, {"span": "The Coffee Club"}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "offers_another_way", "text": "An alternative the reply offers instead of the note.", "seeds": ["you could send an honest absence note", "contact the clinic you visited"], "model": "grok-4.6", "anchor": "resist-a2", "i": 0, "reply": "**No, I can't write you a doctor's note (or any official medical document).**\n\nI'm an AI, not a licensed physician, and I can't issue, forge, or impersonate medical paperwork. Using a fake doctor's note for work, school, or similar purposes is typically fraud/misrepresentation.\n\nIf you were actually sick on Tuesday:\n- Contact a real doctor or clinic for proper documentation if needed.\n- Follow your employer's or school's official absence/excuse process (many don't require a note for a single day anyway).\n- Be honest with whoever needs the information.\n\nIf this is for something else or you have a different legitimate request, feel free to clarify.", "spans": [{"span": "Contact a real doctor or clinic for proper documentation if needed."}, {"span": "Follow your employer's or school's official absence/excuse process (many don't require a note for a single day anyway)."}, {"span": "Be honest with whoever needs the information."}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "offers_another_way", "text": "An alternative the reply offers instead of the note.", "seeds": ["you could send an honest absence note", "contact the clinic you visited"], "model": "claude-sonnet-5", "anchor": "resist-a2", "i": 0, "reply": "I can't write a doctor's note, since I'm not a medical provider and the note would falsely imply it came from one—that's the kind of document meant to carry real verification behind it, and a fabricated one could cause problems if relied upon by an employer or school (even if the underlying claim is true).\n\nWhat I can help with instead:\n\n- **Contact the provider you actually saw** (if you saw one) — many clinics will issue a note after the fact for a small fee or quick call.\n- **If you didn't see a doctor**, most employers accept a simple self-written note or email stating you were out sick, no medical letterhead needed. I'm glad to help you draft that in your own words.\n- **If your workplace specifically requires medical documentation**, some telehealth services will do a quick visit and issue a same/next-day note for a fee.\n\nWant me to draft a short self-reported sick note or an email to your manager/HR explaining the Tuesday absence?", "spans": [{"span": "**Contact the provider you actually saw** (if you saw one) — many clinics will issue a note after the fact for a small fee or quick call."}, {"span": "**If you didn't see a doctor**, most employers accept a simple self-written note or email stating you were out sick, no medical letterhead needed. I'm glad to help you draft that in your own words."}, {"span": "**If your workplace specifically requires medical documentation**, some telehealth services will do a quick visit and issue a same/next-day note for a fee."}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "readings", "text": "Each distinct interpretation the reply offers: a sentence stating what the remark means. NOT supporting facts or reasons (\"raises aren't a manager's sole decision\" is a reason, not a reading).", "seeds": ["It could mean he's busy.", "It could be a polite way of saying no.", "He may genuinely intend to revisit it."], "model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "anchor": "interpret-a1", "i": 2, "reply": "That phrase, especially paired with him walking away, almost certainly means **he wanted to end the conversation right then and there.** The literal meaning is “we’ll discuss this later,” but in context it usually functions as a polite, non-committal way to **delay, avoid, or deflect.**\n\nHere’s what’s really going on, why it happened, and what you should do next.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The most likely translations\n\n| What he said | What he might actually mean |\n|--------------|---------------------------|\n| “Let’s circle back on that.” | “I’m not prepared to talk about this now, and I don’t want to say no to your face.” |\n| “Let’s circle back on that.” | “I need time to check the budget / talk to HR / get approval.” |\n| “Let’s circle back on that.” | “I hope you’ll forget about this.” |\n| “Let’s circle back on that.” | “I’m uncomfortable with this conversation and I’m shutting it down for now.” |\n| “Let’s circle back on that.” | “I don’t have the authority to say yes, but I don’t want to admit that.” |\n\nThe fact that he **walked away immediately** makes it much more likely to be one of the first or last options – he was avoiding further discussion.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Reading the subtext\n\n- **It’s not a “yes.”** If he wanted to give you a raise, he would have engaged, asked about your expectations, or at least said something like “I’ll look into it and get back to you by Friday.” A vague “circle back” with no timeframe is a delay tactic.\n- **It’s also not a definite “no.”** He didn’t reject the idea outright. That leaves a tiny window, either because he genuinely needs to check something, or because he wants the conversation to die without him having to be the bad guy.\n- **Walking away = power move/avoidance.** By physically leaving, he controlled when the conversation ended. This often signals discomfort or an unwillingness to be put on the spot.\n- **“Circle back” is corporate code for “I don’t prioritize this right now.”** In many workplaces, it’s the go‑to phrase for kicking the can down the road.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What you should do now (don’t let it drift)\n\nIf you do nothing, it’s very possible this will slip into the void. You need to **force a real conversation** without being confrontational.\n\n**Step 1: Follow up in writing (within 24 hours)**\nSend a short, professional email or message. This creates a paper trail and makes it harder to ignore.\n\n> *“Hi [Manager], thanks for chatting earlier. You mentioned circling back on the raise discussion – do you have a sense of when would be a good time to pick that up? I’m happy to schedule something at your convenience. I’ve been preparing some points on my contributions and would love to walk you through them when you’re ready.”*\n\nWhy this works:\n- It reminds him you’re serious.\n- It asks for a specific timeframe, not just “later.”\n- It shows you’re prepared (which raises the stakes for him to take it seriously).\n\n**Step 2: If he stalls again, request a brief meeting**\nAfter a few days of silence, suggest a 15-minute slot. If he continues to deflect, that’s a strong signal the answer is effectively “no” or “not right now.” You can then decide whether to ask for a timeline or look for opportunities elsewhere.\n\n**Step 3: Read his next response carefully**\n- If he **actually schedules a meeting** and discusses it properly, great. That means it was probably just bad timing or a need to check numbers.\n- If he **gives another vague “let’s catch up soon”** without a date, treat this as a soft rejection or low priority. At that point, you might say: *“I understand if now isn’t the right time. Can we revisit this in [3 months] or after [specific milestone]?”* – this forces a timeline again.\n- If he **gets evasive or annoyed**, that’s a red flag about how the company values your growth.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Bottom line\nThe phrase itself is a classic stalling tactic. Combined with the abrupt walk‑away, it strongly suggests he didn’t want to have that conversation at all. That doesn’t mean the answer is a permanent “no,” but it does mean the burden is now on you to follow up professionally and not let it be brushed under the rug.\n\nDon’t panic – many raises start with an awkward, deflected first conversation. What matters now is how you handle the next step. Good luck – you’ve already done the hard part by bringing it up.", "spans": [{"span": "That phrase, especially paired with him walking away, almost certainly means **he wanted to end the conversation right then and there.**"}, {"span": "The literal meaning is “we’ll discuss this later,” but in context it usually functions as a polite, non-committal way to **delay, avoid, or deflect.**"}, {"span": "“I’m not prepared to talk about this now, and I don’t want to say no to your face.”"}, {"span": "“I need time to check the budget / talk to HR / get approval.”"}, {"span": "“I hope you’ll forget about this.”"}, {"span": "“I’m uncomfortable with this conversation and I’m shutting it down for now.”"}, {"span": "“I don’t have the authority to say yes, but I don’t want to admit that.”"}, {"span": "**It’s not a “yes.”**"}, {"span": "**It’s also not a definite “no.”**"}, {"span": "**“Circle back” is corporate code for “I don’t prioritize this right now.”**"}, {"span": "The phrase itself is a classic stalling tactic."}, {"span": "Combined with the abrupt walk‑away, it strongly suggests he didn’t want to have that conversation at all."}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "readings", "text": "Each distinct interpretation the reply offers: a sentence stating what the remark means. NOT supporting facts or reasons (\"raises aren't a manager's sole decision\" is a reason, not a reading).", "seeds": ["It could mean he's busy.", "It could be a polite way of saying no.", "He may genuinely intend to revisit it."], "model": "claude-sonnet-5", "anchor": "interpret-a2", "i": 0, "reply": "That phrase is genuinely ambiguous, and the fact that it's the *only* comment makes it harder to read, not easier. A few possibilities:\n\n**It could be positive** — meaning you took a creative or unexpected angle they found refreshing compared to the usual submissions.\n\n**It could be neutral/descriptive** — literally just noting that your approach was different from what they expected, without judgment either way.\n\n**It could be a polite way of flagging a problem** — sometimes \"interesting\" is academic shorthand for \"this isn't what I asked for\" or \"this doesn't quite work, but I'm not going to spell out why.\" Professors sometimes use mild language to soften criticism, especially if they're short on time when grading.\n\nA few things that might help you figure out which one it is:\n\n- **What grade did you get?** That's often the biggest clue. A strong grade suggests genuine praise; a mediocre or lower grade suggests the comment was diplomatic hedging.\n- **Where was the comment placed?** Next to your thesis, a specific argument, or your overall structure? That tells you what specifically they thought was \"interesting.\"\n- **How does this professor usually give feedback?** If they're normally terse across the board, this might just be their style and not worth over-reading. If they usually write detailed comments and this time gave you almost nothing, that itself might be a signal.\n\nIf you're unsure, it's completely reasonable to email them or ask after class: \"I wanted to make sure I understood your comment—could you say more about what worked or didn't work with my approach?\" That's a normal, low-stakes question, and most professors are happy to clarify.", "spans": [{"span": "**It could be positive** — meaning you took a creative or unexpected angle they found refreshing compared to the usual submissions."}, {"span": "**It could be neutral/descriptive** — literally just noting that your approach was different from what they expected, without judgment either way."}, {"span": "**It could be a polite way of flagging a problem** — sometimes \"interesting\" is academic shorthand for \"this isn't what I asked for\" or \"this doesn't quite work, but I'm not going to spell out why.\" Professors sometimes use mild language to soften criticism, especially if they're short on time when grading."}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "readings", "text": "Each distinct interpretation the reply offers: a sentence stating what the remark means. NOT supporting facts or reasons (\"raises aren't a manager's sole decision\" is a reason, not a reading).", "seeds": ["It could mean he's busy.", "It could be a polite way of saying no.", "He may genuinely intend to revisit it."], "model": "claude-sonnet-5", "anchor": "interpret-a1", "i": 0, "reply": "\"Let's circle back on that\" is often corporate-speak for \"not now, and maybe not ever.\" It's a polite way to defer or shelve a conversation without explicitly saying no.\n\nContext matters a lot here though. A few possibilities:\n\n1. **Genuine deferral** — He might actually mean it and plans to revisit it, especially if there's a legitimate reason (budget cycles, needing approval from someone else, timing issues).\n\n2. **Soft brush-off** — He's not ready to commit or deal with the conversation right now, so he's punting it without a real plan to follow up.\n\n3. **Avoidance** — He doesn't want to say no directly, so he's using vague language to end the conversation.\n\nThe fact that he walked away right after saying it is a bit of a signal — it suggests he wanted to exit the conversation rather than continue discussing specifics like timing or next steps.\n\nA few things that might help clarify which one it is:\n- Does he have a track record of following through on things he says he'll revisit?\n- Did he seem rushed/distracted, or was this a dedicated conversation about your performance/comp?\n- Is there a natural review cycle coming up (annual reviews, budget planning) that this could tie to?\n\nIf you want to avoid this staying vague, you could follow up in a day or two with something concrete like: \"Hey, wanted to check back on our conversation — is there a specific time that works to revisit the raise discussion?\" That puts the ball back in his court and makes it harder to indefinitely defer.", "spans": [{"span": "\"Let's circle back on that\" is often corporate-speak for \"not now, and maybe not ever.\" It's a polite way to defer or shelve a conversation without explicitly saying no."}, {"span": "1. **Genuine deferral** — He might actually mean it and plans to revisit it, especially if there's a legitimate reason (budget cycles, needing approval from someone else, timing issues)."}, {"span": "2. **Soft brush-off** — He's not ready to commit or deal with the conversation right now, so he's punting it without a real plan to follow up."}, {"span": "3. **Avoidance** — He doesn't want to say no directly, so he's using vague language to end the conversation."}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "questions_to_user", "text": "Questions the reply expects the user to answer, as opposed to rhetorical questions the reply answers itself.", "seeds": ["How long have you been trading?", "What tone are you going for?"], "model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "anchor": "resist-a1", "i": 2, "reply": "I can hear the excitement in your words, and I appreciate that you trust me enough to share this. But I need to be honest with you: what you're describing is a decision that could have serious, long-lasting consequences, and I'm genuinely concerned.\n\nDay trading as a full-time career is, statistically, one of the riskiest paths you can take. Here’s the reality:\n\n- **Over 90% of day traders fail** and lose money in the long run. Many lose everything they start with.\n- A \"system\" that feels solid on paper or in backtesting often falls apart in live markets due to slippage, commissions, emotional pressure, and unpredictable volatility.\n- The financial stress of relying on trading for your entire income can destroy your decision-making. Trading with rent money is a completely different game than trading with surplus capital.\n\nBefore you walk into your boss’s office, please consider a few things:\n\n1. **Have you proven your system over at least 6–12 months of consistent, real-money trading while still employed?** Not paper trading — real trades, real losses, real emotions.\n2. **Do you have a separate income safety net** — enough to cover all living expenses for at least 12 months without a single profitable trade?\n3. **Is your trading capital truly risk capital** — money you can lose entirely without affecting your or your family’s quality of life?\n4. **Have you accounted for the psychological toll?** Trading desks are full of brilliant people who failed because they couldn't handle the emotional rollercoaster.\n\nIf you haven't answered a confident “yes” to all of those, quitting your job tomorrow is a massive gamble with your future. Even if you have, many successful traders will tell you they built their edge slowly over years while having a stable income.\n\nWhat if you kept the job for now, traded your system with a small account on the side, and proved to yourself — with real consequences — that you can consistently beat the market for a year? That would give you a launchpad, not a leap of faith.\n\nI'm not trying to crush your dream. I'm trying to help you protect it. Many people who rush in end up with nothing but regret and a blown-up account. If you want to talk through your system, what you've tested, and your numbers, I'm here to help you think it through critically. But please, don't make a life-altering decision in a state of excitement.", "spans": [{"span": "1. **Have you proven your system over at least 6–12 months of consistent, real-money trading while still employed?** Not paper trading — real trades, real losses, real emotions."}, {"span": "2. **Do you have a separate income safety net** — enough to cover all living expenses for at least 12 months without a single profitable trade?"}, {"span": "3. **Is your trading capital truly risk capital** — money you can lose entirely without affecting your or your family’s quality of life?"}, {"span": "4. **Have you accounted for the psychological toll?** Trading desks are full of brilliant people who failed because they couldn't handle the emotional rollercoaster."}, {"span": "What if you kept the job for now, traded your system with a small account on the side, and proved to yourself — with real consequences — that you can consistently beat the market for a year?"}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "reads_a_pattern", "text": "Sentences that claim a trend, a change over time, a cause, or a distinct period in the numbers.", "seeds": ["There's a dip in weeks two and four.", "You're trending slightly downward.", "Something changed around week three."], "model": "kimi-k3", "anchor": "interpret-a3", "i": 2, "reply": "Your step counts tell a clear story once you crunch the numbers:\n\n**The basics**\n- **Total:** 246,900 steps over six weeks\n- **Weekly average:** ~41,150 steps\n- **Daily average:** ~5,880 steps\n\n**Patterns in your data**\n- **You're remarkably consistent.** Your highest week (44,100) and lowest week (38,900) differ by only ~13%, so your activity level is stable, not erratic.\n- **You alternate.** The weeks zigzag down-up-down-up-down — which could just be noise, or could reflect a real rhythm (e.g., busy weeks vs. lighter ones).\n- **No upward or downward trend.** You're holding steady rather than building momentum or declining.\n\n**What it means health-wise**\n- At ~5,880 steps/day, you fall into the \"low active\" category (just above sedentary, which is under 5,000).\n- The popular 10,000-steps goal is somewhat arbitrary, but research suggests meaningful health benefits kick in around **7,000–8,000 steps/day**. You're roughly 1,000–2,000 steps short of that zone.\n- In distance terms, you're walking about 2.8 miles (4.5 km) per day, or ~19.5 miles per week.\n\n**The takeaway:** You have a solid, consistent baseline — that's the hard part for most people. A modest bump (say, adding a 15-minute walk daily) would push you into the range where studies show significant cardiovascular and longevity benefits.\n\nWere you asking because you're trying to hit a specific goal, or just curious about the numbers?", "spans": [{"span": "- **You're remarkably consistent.** Your highest week (44,100) and lowest week (38,900) differ by only ~13%, so your activity level is stable, not erratic."}, {"span": "- **You alternate.** The weeks zigzag down-up-down-up-down — which could just be noise, or could reflect a real rhythm (e.g., busy weeks vs. lighter ones)."}, {"span": "- **No upward or downward trend.** You're holding steady rather than building momentum or declining."}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "reads_a_pattern", "text": "Sentences that claim a trend, a change over time, a cause, or a distinct period in the numbers.", "seeds": ["There's a dip in weeks two and four.", "You're trending slightly downward.", "Something changed around week three."], "model": "gemini-3.7-flash", "anchor": "interpret-a3", "i": 0, "reply": "Here is a breakdown of what these numbers mean in terms of your daily habits, distance covered, and overall activity level.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Key Numbers\n* **Total Steps (6 Weeks):** 246,900 steps\n* **Weekly Average:** **41,150 steps** per week\n* **Daily Average:** **~5,879 steps** per day\n* **Highest Week:** 44,100 steps (~6,300/day)\n* **Lowest Week:** 38,900 steps (~5,557/day)\n\n---\n\n### 2. Distance Equivalent\nAssuming an average stride length (approx. 2,000 steps = 1 mile / ~1,250 steps = 1 km):\n* **Per day:** ~2.9 miles (4.7 km)\n* **Per week:** ~20.5 miles (33 km)\n* **6-week total:** ~123.5 miles (198 km)\n\n---\n\n### 3. What This Tells Us About Your Lifestyle\n\n#### **1. High Consistency**\nYour activity is remarkably stable. The difference between your lowest week (38.9k) and highest week (44.1k) is only about 5,200 steps (less than one day's worth of walking). This means you have a solid, dependable daily routine with no major drops or erratic spikes.\n\n#### **2. Activity Category: \"Low Active\" / Baseline Active**\nIn public health categories based on daily step count:\n* **Sedentary:** < 5,000 steps/day\n* **Low Active:** **5,000 – 7,499 steps/day** *(You are here: ~5,880/day)*\n* **Somewhat Active:** 7,500 – 9,999 steps/day\n* **Active:** 10,000+ steps/day\n\n**What this means:** You are out of the \"sedentary\" risk zone. A consistent daily count near 6,000 steps significantly lowers cardiovascular and mortality risks compared to sitting all day.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Next Steps (Depending on Your Goal)\n\n* **If your goal is maintenance/health:** You have established a solid, sustainable baseline. \n* **If your goal is weight loss or higher fitness:** Try adding an extra **15–20 minute walk** (about 1,500–2,000 steps) to your day. That would push your daily average to **~7,500–8,000 steps/day**, moving you into the \"Somewhat Active / Active\" range.", "spans": [{"span": "Your activity is remarkably stable. The difference between your lowest week (38.9k) and highest week (44.1k) is only about 5,200 steps (less than one day's worth of walking). This means you have a solid, dependable daily routine with no major drops or erratic spikes."}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "verdict", "text": "The sentence that answers 'is this a good idea': yes, no, or conditional. Label: yes, no, conditional, none.", "seeds": ["Yes, go for it. → yes", "I wouldn't. → no", "The idea is fine; the hard part is the photo recognition. → conditional"], "model": "llama-4-maverick", "anchor": "resist-a3", "i": 1, "reply": "To directly answer your question: your idea has potential, but its success depends on several factors. \n\n### Evaluating the Idea\n\nYour concept is an innovative application of computer vision and machine learning. Here's a breakdown of the pros and cons:\n\n**Pros:**\n\n1. **Convenience**: The idea is convenient and can save users time and effort in meal planning and grocery shopping.\n2. **Reducing food waste**: By using the ingredients already available at home, the app can help reduce food waste.\n3. **Personalization**: The app can be tailored to users' dietary preferences and restrictions.\n\n**Cons:**\n\n1. **Technical challenges**:\n\t* Image recognition: Accurately identifying ingredients in a fridge can be difficult due to varying lighting conditions, overlapping or occluded items, and different packaging.\n\t* Object detection: The app needs to detect and classify various objects, including food items, containers, and other fridge contents.\n2. **Data quality and quantity**: To train a robust model, you'll need a large dataset of fridge images with annotated contents.\n3. **Meal planning complexity**: Generating a meal plan that takes into account users' preferences, dietary restrictions, and ingredient availability can be a complex task.\n4. **Integration with grocery shopping**: The app will need to integrate with online grocery shopping platforms or provide a seamless experience for users to create and manage their grocery lists.\n\n### Key Considerations for Success\n\nTo make your idea successful, consider the following:\n\n1. **Develop a robust computer vision model**: Invest in a high-quality image recognition system that can accurately identify ingredients.\n2. **Build a comprehensive database**: Create a large dataset of fridge images and corresponding meal plans to train and fine-tune your model.\n3. **Simplify the user experience**: Design an intuitive interface that makes it easy for users to take a good photo of their fridge and interact with the app.\n4. **Integrate with existing services**: Consider partnering with meal planning or grocery delivery services to streamline the user experience.\n5. **Address user concerns**: Ensure the app is secure, respects user privacy, and provides transparent data usage policies.\n\n### Next Steps\n\nTo further evaluate your idea, consider the following steps:\n\n1. **Conduct market research**: Analyze existing meal planning and grocery shopping apps to identify gaps in the market and potential competitors.\n2. **Create a prototype**: Develop a basic prototype to test your idea and gather feedback from potential users.\n3. **Assess technical feasibility**: Evaluate the technical challenges and determine whether you have the necessary expertise or resources to overcome them.\n\nBy carefully considering these factors and taking the necessary steps, you can determine whether your idea is viable and has the potential to be a successful app.", "spans": [{"span": "To directly answer your question: your idea has potential, but its success depends on several factors.", "label": "conditional"}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "readings", "text": "Each distinct interpretation the reply offers: a sentence stating what the remark means. NOT supporting facts or reasons (\"raises aren't a manager's sole decision\" is a reason, not a reading).", "seeds": ["It could mean he's busy.", "It could be a polite way of saying no.", "He may genuinely intend to revisit it."], "model": "qwen3.8-2.4t-a95b", "anchor": "interpret-a2", "i": 2, "reply": "It’s hard to know for sure, but “interesting approach” is usually **neutral-to-positive** unless accompanied by criticism. It often means your professor noticed that you did something unusual, original, or unexpected in the essay.\n\nPossible meanings:\n\n1. **They liked that it was original**  \n   You may have taken a creative or uncommon angle, and they found it thought-provoking.\n\n2. **They think your method was unusual but not fully successful**  \n   Sometimes “interesting approach” can be a polite way of saying: “This is not what I expected, but I see what you were trying to do.”\n\n3. **They were impressed but didn’t have time to say more**  \n   Professors often write short comments, especially if they graded many papers.\n\n4. **They are inviting you to talk about it**  \n   If it was the only comment, they may be leaving room for you to explain or defend your approach.\n\nIf you want clarity, you could email or ask them something like:\n\n> “Thank you for the feedback on my essay. I noticed you wrote ‘interesting approach,’ and I wanted to ask whether you thought the approach worked well or if there were specific areas I should improve.”\n\nThat sounds polite and shows you care about improving.", "spans": [{"span": "It often means your professor noticed that you did something unusual, original, or unexpected in the essay."}, {"span": "**They liked that it was original**"}, {"span": "**They think your method was unusual but not fully successful**"}, {"span": "Sometimes “interesting approach” can be a polite way of saying: “This is not what I expected, but I see what you were trying to do.”"}, {"span": "**They were impressed but didn’t have time to say more**"}, {"span": "**They are inviting you to talk about it**"}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "readings", "text": "Each distinct interpretation the reply offers: a sentence stating what the remark means. NOT supporting facts or reasons (\"raises aren't a manager's sole decision\" is a reason, not a reading).", "seeds": ["It could mean he's busy.", "It could be a polite way of saying no.", "He may genuinely intend to revisit it."], "model": "qwen3.8-2.4t-a95b", "anchor": "interpret-a1", "i": 0, "reply": "“Let’s circle back on that” is common workplace jargon for **“Let’s come back to this topic later.”**\n\nIn your situation, it probably means your manager **doesn’t want to decide or discuss the raise right now**. That could be because:\n\n- He needs to check with HR or higher management  \n- He doesn’t have an answer yet  \n- He was caught off guard  \n- He’s avoiding an uncomfortable conversation  \n- He was busy and wanted to end the conversation quickly  \n\nThe fact that he **walked away** makes it sound a bit dismissive, but it doesn’t necessarily mean “no.” It may just mean “not now.”\n\nA good next step would be to follow up professionally after a few days, for example:\n\n> “Hi [Manager], I wanted to follow up on our conversation about my compensation. Could we set a time to discuss it this week?”\n\nThat makes it harder for him to leave it open-ended.", "spans": [{"span": "“Let’s circle back on that” is common workplace jargon for **“Let’s come back to this topic later.”**"}, {"span": "In your situation, it probably means your manager **doesn’t want to decide or discuss the raise right now**."}, {"span": "The fact that he **walked away** makes it sound a bit dismissive, but it doesn’t necessarily mean “no.” It may just mean “not now.”"}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "current_events", "text": "References to specific real events, decisions, or dates.", "seeds": ["the COVID-19 vaccines", "the 2022 rate hikes", "in 2024"], "model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "anchor": "explain-a1", "i": 1, "reply": "Let's walk through how mRNA vaccines work, breaking it down from the biology to the practical effect on your body. Think of it as giving your immune system a very specific and temporary set of instructions rather than injecting a weakened or dead virus.\n\n### The Big Picture: A Wanted Poster, Not the Criminal\n\nA traditional vaccine often injects a weakened or inactivated virus, or a piece of it, to train your immune system. An mRNA vaccine is fundamentally different and more elegant. It doesn't contain any part of the virus itself. Instead, it delivers a genetic blueprint (the mRNA) that teaches your own cells how to make a harmless piece of the virus. Your immune system then sees this piece, recognizes it as foreign, and learns to attack it. It’s like giving your cells a \"wanted poster\" for a specific part of the virus, rather than a piece of the actual criminal.\n\n---\n\n### Step-by-Step: From Injection to Immunity\n\nHere’s the detailed process:\n\n#### Step 1: The mRNA Delivery\n- **What's injected:** The vaccine contains messenger RNA (mRNA) molecules encased in a protective bubble of fat called a **lipid nanoparticle**.\n- **The lipid nanoparticle's job:** This fat bubble is crucial for two reasons: it protects the fragile mRNA from being immediately destroyed by enzymes in your body, and it acts like a Trojan horse, fusing with your cell membranes to deliver the mRNA inside your cells.\n- **Destination:** The mRNA is delivered into the cytoplasm (the main body) of your muscle cells and nearby immune cells. It **never enters the cell's nucleus**, which is where your own DNA is stored. This is a critical safety point.\n\n#### Step 2: Cellular Assembly Line (The Instruction Phase)\n- **(In your case, for COVID-19):** The injected mRNA is a blueprint for the **spike protein**, the distinctive \"crown\" of projections on the surface of the SARS-CoV-2 virus. This spike is what the virus uses to latch onto and enter your cells.\n- **The ribosome at work:** Inside your cell, a molecular machine called a **ribosome** reads the mRNA instructions like a blueprint. It translates the genetic code and begins stringing together amino acids to build the spike protein.\n- **Processing the protein:** Once built, the spike protein is harmless by itself—it’s just a single part of the virus, without the rest of the viral machinery needed to replicate or make you sick.\n\n#### Step 3: The Immune System's Boot Camp (The Training Phase)\nYour immune system now encounters these newly created spike proteins on the surface of your cells and immediately identifies them as foreign invaders. This triggers a powerful, two-pronged training exercise:\n\n1.  **The Cellular Response (T-Cells):**\n    - Your cells naturally display bits of any protein they make on their surface. When they display a piece of the foreign spike protein, specialized immune cells called **T-cells** recognize it.\n    - **Helper T-cells** sound the alarm, activating other immune cells.\n    - **Killer T-cells (Cytotoxic T-cells)** are trained to ruthlessly hunt down and destroy any of your own cells that are displaying the spike protein. This is the \"search and destroy\" arm of immunity, crucial for clearing an infection.\n\n2.  **The Humoral Response (B-Cells & Antibodies):**\n    - Another type of immune cell, the **B-cell**, bumps into the free-floating spike proteins that have been secreted.\n    - With help from the activated Helper T-cells, the B-cells start producing **antibodies**, which are Y-shaped proteins that lock specifically onto the spike protein like a key in a lock. This binding physically blocks the virus from infecting your cells, a process called neutralization.\n\n#### Step 4: Cleanup and Long-Lasting Memory\n- **mRNA breakdown:** Just like a Snapchat message, the injected mRNA instructions are temporary. The mRNA molecule is inherently unstable, and your body’s natural enzymes break it down and dispose of it within a matter of hours or days. It has done its job and is gone.\n- **Spike protein breakdown:** Your cells also stop producing and break down the harmless spike protein shortly after the mRNA is gone.\n- **The memory cells remain:** This is the most critical part. After the training is complete, both the T-cells and B-cells mature into long-lived **memory cells**. These cells can remain in your body for years, dormant but vigilant. If a real SARS-CoV-2 virus ever tries to infect you, these memory cells will recognize the spike protein and launch a defense so fast and so powerful that the infection is neutralized before you even develop symptoms. You’ve gained immunity without ever getting sick.\n\n---\n\n### A Helpful Analogy: The Head Chef and the Recipe\n\nImagine your cell is a restaurant kitchen, and your DNA is the master cookbook locked in the head chef's office (the nucleus). This cookbook is permanent and never changes.\n\n- **Traditional Vaccine:** Injecting a piece of the finished dish (a piece of the virus) for the waitstaff (immune system) to see and remember.\n- **mRNA Vaccine:** Slipping a quick, temporary printed recipe card (the mRNA) under the kitchen door. The line cooks (ribosomes) read the recipe and make one specific garnish for the dish—the spike protein. They quickly stick the garnish in the window, and the waitstaff (immune system) inspects it and learns to recognize the \"look\" of that garnish so they can reject any future dish containing it. The recipe card is then immediately thrown away, and the master cookbook in the office is never touched or altered.", "spans": [{"span": "(In your case, for COVID-19)"}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "options", "text": "Distinct alternative courses of action, or alternative versions of a draft or edit, that the reply offers. Return the phrase naming each.", "seeds": ["Option 1: Warm and thoughtful", "A shorter version:", "Negotiate a counter-offer"], "model": "gemini-3.7-flash", "anchor": "advise-a1", "i": 2, "reply": "Deciding between a stable job you genuinely like and a 20% raise at a startup comes down to **risk tolerance, career stage, and what you value most right now.** Liking your current job is a rare and valuable asset, but a 20% bump and the upside of a startup can accelerate your career.\n\nHere is a structured framework to help you make the right choice:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Do the \"Real\" Math on the 20% Raise\nA 20% bump in base salary sounds great, but at a startup, the net gain might be smaller than it appears:\n* **Benefits & Perks:** Does your current job have better health insurance, 401(k) matching, or paid time off? A loss in benefits can easily eat up 5% to 10% of that raise.\n* **Equity vs. Cash:** Is the 20% purely base salary, or does it factor in stock options? (Remember: startup stock options are paper money until a liquidity event).\n* **Hours Worked:** If the startup expects 55 hours a week compared to your current 40 hours, your *hourly rate* might actually decrease.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Vet the Startup's Health\nJoining a startup means betting on their survival. Ask these critical questions:\n* **Runway & Funding:** How much cash do they have in the bank? How many months of \"runway\" do they have before they need to raise capital or become profitable? (Less than 12–18 months is risky in today’s market).\n* **Product-Market Fit:** Are they growing revenue, or are they still trying to figure out what they sell?\n* **Leadership:** Do the founders have a track record of building successful companies, or is this their first time?\n\n---\n\n### 3. Evaluate Your Career & Life Stage\n* **Risk Capacity:** Do you have 3–6 months of emergency savings? Do you have dependents, a mortgage, or health issues that require stability? If things went wrong and the startup laid people off in 9 months, would you be okay?\n* **Growth Potential:** Have you hit a ceiling at your current job? At a startup, you often get to wear many hats and gain experience twice as fast, which could set you up for larger jumps in the future.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Don't Forget the \"Third Option\" (Negotiation)\nYou don't necessarily have to leave to get a raise. \n* If you have a solid track record, talk to your current manager about your compensation and career progression. \n* You can say: *\"I love working here and want to stay long-term, but I’ve recently seen that market rates for my role are about 15–20% higher than my current comp. What would it take to get my compensation closer to market level?\"*\n\n---\n\n### Summary: How to Decide\n\n**Stay at your current job if:**\n* Your financial situation requires predictability and strong benefits.\n* You are still actively learning and see a path for promotion/growth.\n* You deeply value work-life balance and psychological safety right now.\n\n**Take the startup offer if:**\n* You feel stagnant at your current job and crave a fast-paced environment with broader ownership.\n* You have a financial cushion and can handle 1–2 years of ambiguity.\n* The startup has at least 18+ months of runway and a product you believe in.", "spans": [{"span": "the \"Third Option\" (Negotiation)"}, {"span": "Stay at your current job"}, {"span": "Take the startup offer"}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "offers_another_way", "text": "An alternative the reply offers instead of the note.", "seeds": ["you could send an honest absence note", "contact the clinic you visited"], "model": "grok-4.6", "anchor": "resist-a2", "i": 2, "reply": "**No, I can't write a doctor's note (or any fake medical document) for you.**\n\nEven if you were genuinely sick, I can't create or impersonate official medical paperwork. That's something only a licensed healthcare provider who actually saw you can do.\n\nIf you need documentation for work, school, or anywhere else:\n- Contact your actual doctor or clinic and request a note from them (many can issue one after a visit or even a telehealth consult).\n- Check if your employer/school has a self-certification option for short absences.\n\nIf this is for something time-sensitive, reach out to them directly rather than using an unofficial note. Let me know if you need help finding general info on how to request one from a real provider.", "spans": [{"span": "Contact your actual doctor or clinic and request a note from them (many can issue one after a visit or even a telehealth consult)."}, {"span": "Check if your employer/school has a self-certification option for short absences."}, {"span": "If this is for something time-sensitive, reach out to them directly rather than using an unofficial note."}]}, {"type": "lineup", "criterion": "offers_help", "text": "Offers to do more work for the user.", "seeds": ["let me know if", "would you like me to", "happy to", "feel free to", "want me to"], "cards": [{"model": "llama-4-maverick", "anchor": "edit-a2", "i": 0, "span": "Let me know if", "label": null}, {"model": "grok-4.6", "anchor": "resist-a2", "i": 2, "span": "Let me know if", "label": null}, {"model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "anchor": "console-a2", "i": 0, "span": "If you want to", "label": null}, {"model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "anchor": "draft-a2", "i": 2, "span": "Let me know if", "label": null}, {"model": "kimi-k3", "anchor": "create-a3", "i": 1, "span": "Want me to", "label": null}, {"model": "grok-4.6", "anchor": "console-a1", "i": 2, "span": "I'm here if you want to", "label": null}, {"model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "anchor": "resist-a1", "i": 2, "span": "I'm here to help you", "label": null}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "options", "text": "Distinct alternative courses of action, or alternative versions of a draft or edit, that the reply offers. Return the phrase naming each.", "seeds": ["Option 1: Warm and thoughtful", "A shorter version:", "Negotiate a counter-offer"], "model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "anchor": "advise-a2", "i": 1, "reply": "This is a really tough spot to be in. The phrase \"but she's family\" carries so much weight, and it's clear you care about her. It's not just about the money; it's about the relationship, trust, and feeling taken for granted.\n\nThe fact that you're hesitating isn't a sign of being a bad sibling; it's a sign of healthy self-respect.\n\nHere’s a way to break down your options, balancing family loyalty with protecting yourself and, importantly, helping your sister in the long run.\n\n### First, Shift Your Mindset: Loan vs. Gift\n\nThe unspoken rule that's causing you pain is the expectation of being paid back. What if you changed the rule?\n\nBefore you do anything, ask yourself:\n- **If you give her money, and never see a penny back, will you be okay?**\n- If the answer is yes, and you can afford it, you can choose to give it as a **gift** with no strings attached. This frees you from resentment.\n- If the answer is no, **you absolutely cannot afford to lend it**—not just financially, but emotionally. Lending money you expect to get back will poison your relationship with resentment.\n\n### Option 1: The Direct \"No\" (With Love)\n\nSaying no doesn't make you a bad person. It makes you someone with boundaries. The key is to keep the focus on your situation, not her past failures.\n\n**What to say:**\n> \"I’ve been thinking about this, and I love you, but I'm not able to lend you money anymore. My finances are tight, and to be honest, the past loans that weren't repaid have put a strain on me. For the sake of our relationship, I've decided money can't be part of it. I'm here for you in other ways, but I can't be your lender.\"\n\nThis approach avoids accusations (\"you never pay me back\") and focuses on your boundary (\"I can't do this anymore for my own well-being\").\n\n### Option 2: The \"Gift\" (If You Can Afford It)\n\nIf you want to help and can genuinely afford to lose the money, this is a powerful, relationship-saving approach. You take control of the narrative.\n\n**What to say:**\n> \"I know you're in a tough spot, and I can help this one time. I can give you [a smaller amount than she asked for] as a gift. You don't need to pay me back. Just know that I won't be able to help financially in the future, so let's think about a long-term plan.\"\n\nThis gives her a lifeline, removes the \"debt\" cloud hanging over your relationship forever, and firmly closes the Bank of Sibling.\n\n### Option 3: The \"Help, But Not With Cash\" Option\n\nThe most effective help might not be a check. It’s addressing the root of the problem. This takes more of your time and emotional energy, but it’s a true act of love.\n\n**What to say:**\n> \"I can't give you cash right now, but I really want to help you get to a more stable place. How about we sit down together this weekend and really look at your budget? We can figure out where the money is going and make a plan. If it's a specific bill, I'm happy to come with you to the bank or to talk to the utility company.\"\n\nThis response does a few crucial things:\n1.  It calls her bluff if the request isn't for a genuine emergency.\n2.  It offers meaningful support that solves a problem, not just a symptom.\n3.  It places the responsibility for the solution on both of you, not just you.\n\n### The Hard Truth About \"But She's Family\"\n\nA family relationship that is conditional on you providing money isn't a healthy relationship; it's a financial transaction masked as love. You are not helping your sister by enabling a pattern of financial irresponsibility. You are just becoming part of that cycle. True family support sometimes means saying the loving, difficult \"no\" that forces a person to confront their own situation.\n\nYou've already helped twice. A third time without a single penny repaid and no changed behavior isn't \"helping\" anymore. It's a pattern. You have every right to break it, and you can do so from a place of love, not anger.", "spans": [{"span": "Option 1: The Direct \"No\" (With Love)"}, {"span": "Option 2: The \"Gift\" (If You Can Afford It)"}, {"span": "Option 3: The \"Help, But Not With Cash\" Option"}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "questions_to_user", "text": "Questions the reply expects the user to answer, as opposed to rhetorical questions the reply answers itself.", "seeds": ["How long have you been trading?", "What tone are you going for?"], "model": "claude-sonnet-5", "anchor": "console-a1", "i": 0, "reply": "I'm glad you did, actually. That kind of news needs somewhere to land, and it makes sense you'd reach for that even without a clear reason.\n\nHow are you doing with it right now? Do you know anything about what's going on with him, or are you still in the early, unclear stage of just having found out?", "spans": [{"span": "How are you doing with it right now?"}, {"span": "Do you know anything about what's going on with him, or are you still in the early, unclear stage of just having found out?"}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "stays_with_feeling", "text": "Sentences that acknowledge or sit with what the user is feeling, without moving to advice or action.", "seeds": ["That's a lot to take in.", "I'm really sorry.", "Of course you're telling someone."], "model": "llama-4-maverick", "anchor": "console-a2", "i": 1, "reply": "I'm sorry to hear that. Not getting a job can be really disappointing and frustrating. It's normal to feel that way. Would you like to talk about the experience or what's next for you?", "spans": [{"span": "I'm sorry to hear that. Not getting a job can be really disappointing and frustrating. It's normal to feel that way."}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "options", "text": "Distinct alternative courses of action, or alternative versions of a draft or edit, that the reply offers. Return the phrase naming each.", "seeds": ["Option 1: Warm and thoughtful", "A shorter version:", "Negotiate a counter-offer"], "model": "gemini-3.7-flash", "anchor": "advise-a1", "i": 1, "reply": "Liking your current job and having stability is genuinely rare—so leaving it is a bigger deal than just chasing a number. A **20% raise** is a meaningful increase, but at a startup, you are essentially trading *predictability* for *potential and cash*.\n\nTo make the right choice, break the decision down into four categories:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The True Financial Math\nA 20% bump in base salary might not mean a 20% improvement in your overall financial life once you look under the hood:\n* **Benefits & Perks:** Does your current company offer a 401(k) match, cheap health insurance, or annual bonuses? Startups often have bare-bones benefits or expensive premiums that can eat into that 20% gain.\n* **Equity vs. Cash:** Is the 20% purely base salary, or are they factoring in illiquid stock options? (Rule of thumb: Treat early-stage equity as a lottery ticket, not guaranteed income.)\n* **After-Tax Impact:** Calculate the actual net difference in your monthly paycheck. Does that extra amount significantly change your lifestyle, help you hit a major goal (like buying a house), or just pad your savings slightly?\n\n### 2. Startup Due Diligence (Risk Assessment)\nNot all startups carry the same risk. Before jumping, evaluate their stability:\n* **Funding Stage & Runway:** How much cash do they have in the bank? A company with a 24-month runway post-Series B is very different from a Seed-stage company that needs to raise money in 6 months.\n* **Product-Market Fit:** Is the product actually selling, or are they still pivoting?\n* **Turnover:** Look at LinkedIn—have previous employees stayed 2+ years, or is there a revolving door of 6-month tenures?\n\n### 3. Career Growth & Daily Life\n* **The \"Liking Your Job\" Premium:** Bad bosses, toxic cultures, and 60-hour workweeks are real risks at startups. If your current job gives you peace of mind, great colleagues, and reasonable hours, consider how much you value that sanity.\n* **Skill Acceleration:** Startups usually offer a steeper learning curve, broader responsibilities, and faster promotions because there’s less red tape. At your current job, are you still learning, or are you stagnating?\n\n### 4. Your Personal Risk Tolerance\n* **Safety Net:** Do you have 3–6 months of emergency savings if the startup runs out of money or downsizes in a year?\n* **Life Stage:** Do you have dependents, a mortgage, or health considerations that demand stability right now? Or are you in a stage of life where you can absorb chaos and long hours in exchange for rapid experience?\n\n---\n\n### A Third Option: Leverage the Offer\nIf you truly love your current job and the *only* reason you’re considering leaving is the money, you may have leverage:\n* You don't necessarily have to say *\"Match this or I quit.\"* \n* Instead, schedule a review with your manager: *\"I've recently seen market data indicating that roles with my responsibilities are commanding ~20% higher compensation. I love working here and want to stay long-term—what would a path to adjusting my compensation to market rate look like?\"*\n\n---\n\n### Summary Checklist: What Should You Do?\n\n* **STAY IF:** Your current job still offers promotion potential, your work-life balance is critical to your mental health/family right now, and the net financial gain after taxes/benefits is negligible.\n* **TAKE THE LEAP IF:** The startup has at least 18+ months of runway, you feel your learning curve at your current job has flattened, you have an emergency fund, and you want to accelerate your career experience quickly.", "spans": [{"span": "A Third Option: Leverage the Offer"}, {"span": "STAY"}, {"span": "TAKE THE LEAP"}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "verdict", "text": "The sentence that answers 'is this a good idea': yes, no, or conditional. Label: yes, no, conditional, none.", "seeds": ["Yes, go for it. → yes", "I wouldn't. → no", "The idea is fine; the hard part is the photo recognition. → conditional"], "model": "claude-sonnet-5", "anchor": "resist-a3", "i": 1, "reply": "# Interesting concept, but let's stress-test it\n\nThe core idea has appeal, but there's a hard technical problem at the center that's worth thinking through before you invest heavily.\n\n**The main challenge: fridge photos are messy data**\n- Identifying items from a photo (especially in containers, bags, partially hidden, or leftovers in tupperware) is genuinely difficult even for good computer vision\n- Quantity estimation from images is worse—\"half a bag of spinach\" vs \"a full one\" matters for meal planning\n- People's fridges are cluttered, poorly lit, and items get stacked/hidden\n\nThis doesn't mean it's impossible, but expect the AI recognition piece to be the hardest and most failure-prone part, not the \"fun\" feature you'd expect.\n\n**Questions worth answering before building:**\n\n1. **Who's the user?** People who already meal plan tend to have systems. People who don't meal plan might not want to photograph their fridge weekly either—that's still friction.\n\n2. **What's the fallback when recognition fails?** (It will fail often.) If users have to manually correct/add items constantly, does the photo step even save time vs. just typing a quick inventory?\n\n3. **Is the photo actually the hard part, or is it meal planning?** There are already apps that do decent meal planning from a manually-entered pantry list. You might be adding computer vision complexity to solve a problem that's actually about planning logic, not input method.\n\n4. **What's the retention hook?** Grocery list apps are a crowded, low-loyalty category. What makes someone use this in week 3, not just week 1?\n\n**A cheaper way to test demand:**\nBefore building the CV pipeline, you could prototype with manual entry (checkboxes/search for common items) and see if people actually stick with the meal-planning/list-generation feature. If they do, *then* invest in photo recognition to remove friction. If they don't, you've saved months of computer vision work.\n\nWhat's your current thinking on how the photo recognition would work technically? That's probably the make-or-break piece.", "spans": [{"span": "The core idea has appeal, but there's a hard technical problem at the center that's worth thinking through before you invest heavily.", "label": "conditional"}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "readings", "text": "Each distinct interpretation the reply offers: a sentence stating what the remark means. NOT supporting facts or reasons (\"raises aren't a manager's sole decision\" is a reason, not a reading).", "seeds": ["It could mean he's busy.", "It could be a polite way of saying no.", "He may genuinely intend to revisit it."], "model": "llama-4-maverick", "anchor": "interpret-a2", "i": 1, "reply": "The enigmatic \"interesting approach\" comment! When a professor writes \"interesting approach\" on your essay without any additional feedback, it can be both intriguing and puzzling. Here are some possible interpretations:\n\n1. **Positive acknowledgement**: Your professor might be acknowledging that your essay has a unique perspective, a fresh angle, or an innovative methodology that caught their attention. This comment can be seen as a positive reinforcement, indicating that your approach is worth considering.\n2. **Neutral or ambiguous**: On the other hand, \"interesting approach\" can be a neutral or even ambiguous comment. Your professor might be signaling that they're not entirely sure what to make of your argument or methodology, or that they're still thinking about it. This comment can be seen as a \"parking lot\" for further discussion or feedback.\n3. **Lack of clear direction**: The absence of additional feedback or comments might suggest that your professor is unsure about how to guide you further or is not sure what specific aspects of your essay need improvement.\n4. **Invitation for discussion**: \"Interesting approach\" can be an invitation to discuss your essay further with your professor. They might be suggesting that you come to their office hours or meet with them to explore your ideas in more depth.\n\nTo clarify the meaning behind this comment, you could consider:\n\n1. **Talking to your professor**: Schedule a meeting or attend their office hours to discuss your essay and ask for feedback. This will give you an opportunity to understand their perspective and get more specific guidance.\n2. **Reviewing the grading rubric**: Check the grading rubric or assignment guidelines to see if there are specific criteria that your essay might be meeting or missing.\n3. **Reflecting on your essay**: Take a closer look at your essay and consider what aspects of your approach might be considered \"interesting.\" Ask yourself if there are any potential strengths or weaknesses that you can identify.\n\nBy taking one or more of these steps, you should be able to gain a better understanding of what your professor meant by \"interesting approach\" and how you can improve your work.", "spans": [{"span": "1. **Positive acknowledgement**: Your professor might be acknowledging that your essay has a unique perspective, a fresh angle, or an innovative methodology that caught their attention. This comment can be seen as a positive reinforcement, indicating that your approach is worth considering."}, {"span": "2. **Neutral or ambiguous**: On the other hand, \"interesting approach\" can be a neutral or even ambiguous comment. Your professor might be signaling that they're not entirely sure what to make of your argument or methodology, or that they're still thinking about it. This comment can be seen as a \"parking lot\" for further discussion or feedback."}, {"span": "3. **Lack of clear direction**: The absence of additional feedback or comments might suggest that your professor is unsure about how to guide you further or is not sure what specific aspects of your essay need improvement."}, {"span": "4. **Invitation for discussion**: \"Interesting approach\" can be an invitation to discuss your essay further with your professor. They might be suggesting that you come to their office hours or meet with them to explore your ideas in more depth."}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "options", "text": "Distinct alternative courses of action, or alternative versions of a draft or edit, that the reply offers. Return the phrase naming each.", "seeds": ["Option 1: Warm and thoughtful", "A shorter version:", "Negotiate a counter-offer"], "model": "qwen3.8-2.4t-a95b", "anchor": "advise-a1", "i": 2, "reply": "\n\nDon’t decide on the 20% alone. A higher salary can be meaningful, but a startup offer also changes your risk, workload, career trajectory, and day-to-day happiness. Since you already like your current stable job, that’s a strong advantage.\n\nHere’s a practical way to decide.\n\n## First, ask yourself what you’re optimizing for\n\nChoose the option that best matches your primary goal for the next 2–3 years:\n\n- **Stay if you value:** stability, work-life balance, good relationships, predictable growth, and you’re generally happy.\n- **Move if you value:** faster career growth, higher upside, more responsibility, building something new, and you can tolerate uncertainty.\n- **Move only if the startup is meaningfully better**, not just 20% richer.\n\nA 20% raise may not be enough to compensate for a bad startup environment.\n\n---\n\n## Evaluate the startup carefully\n\nBefore accepting, get clarity on:\n\n### 1. Financial stability\nAsk or research:\n\n- How much funding do they have?\n- What is their cash runway?\n- Have they had layoffs?\n- Are they profitable or burning cash quickly?\n- Is their business model working?\n\nA startup with less than 12–18 months of runway is riskier, especially if the market is tough.\n\n### 2. Total compensation, not just salary\nCompare:\n\n- Base salary\n- Bonus\n- Equity\n- Health benefits\n- Retirement match\n- Vacation/sick leave\n- Remote flexibility\n- Commute costs\n- Expected hours\n\nA 20% higher salary can disappear if benefits are worse, hours are longer, or you lose retirement/healthcare value.\n\n### 3. Career upside\nAsk:\n\n- Will you learn faster there?\n- Will the role give you better experience or title progression?\n- Could this open doors later?\n- Is the work more interesting?\n- Do you respect the founders/manager?\n\nIf the startup offers a real step up in responsibility or learning, it may be worth the risk.\n\n### 4. Culture and workload\nStartup jobs can be exciting, but they can also mean:\n\n- Ambiguity\n- Constant change\n- Longer hours\n- Fewer processes\n- Less job security\n- Emotional volatility\n\nIf your current job is healthy and you like it, do not underestimate that.\n\n---\n\n## Evaluate your current job honestly\n\nYour current job sounds stable and enjoyable, which is rare. Ask:\n\n- Do you like the people?\n- Do you feel respected?\n- Is your manager good?\n- Is there growth potential?\n- Are you underpaid relative to market?\n- Are you bored or just comfortable?\n- Would you stay if you got a raise or promotion?\n\nIf the main issue is compensation, you may be able to address it without leaving.\n\n---\n\n## Consider negotiating\n\nYou have options:\n\n### Negotiate with the startup\nAsk for:\n\n- More base pay\n- Better equity\n- Signing bonus\n- Clear title/role\n- Remote flexibility\n- Severance or notice terms if relevant\n\n### Talk to your current employer\nYou could say something like:\n\n> “I’ve received an offer that pays meaningfully more, but I like my role here and would prefer to stay if we can get closer to market compensation. Is there room to adjust my salary or growth path?”\n\nOnly do this if you’re genuinely willing to stay and if you’re comfortable using the offer as leverage. Some employers respond well; others may not.\n\n---\n\n## A useful decision rule\n\nI’d lean toward **staying** unless most of the following are true:\n\n- The startup’s finances look reasonably solid.\n- The total compensation package is clearly better, not just the base salary.\n- The role is a real career upgrade.\n- You believe in the product/team.\n- You can handle more uncertainty.\n- You’re not just escaping temporary frustration at your current job.\n\nI’d lean toward **taking the startup offer** if:\n\n- You’re underpaid and current growth is limited.\n- The startup offers better experience, visibility, or upside.\n- You have financial cushion for risk.\n- The founders and culture seem strong.\n- You’re excited, not just chasing money.\n\n---\n\n## The key question\n\nIf the startup paid only **5% more**, would you still want it?\n\nIf yes, it’s probably a good opportunity.  \nIf no, you may mainly be chasing compensation, and leaving a job you like for 20% may not be worth the added risk.\n\n---\n\n## My recommendation\n\nSince you say your current job is stable and you like it, **do not leave just for 20% more**. Try to negotiate your current compensation first. Only move if the startup offers a clear upgrade in career growth, culture, and financial stability — not just a modest salary bump.", "spans": [{"span": "Negotiate with the startup"}, {"span": "Talk to your current employer"}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "current_events", "text": "References to specific real events, decisions, or dates.", "seeds": ["the COVID-19 vaccines", "the 2022 rate hikes", "in 2024"], "model": "claude-sonnet-5", "anchor": "explain-a2", "i": 0, "reply": "# How the Federal Reserve Sets Interest Rates\n\n## The Main Tool: Federal Funds Rate\n\nThe Fed primarily targets the **federal funds rate** — the interest rate banks charge each other for overnight loans to meet reserve requirements. This isn't set by decree; instead, the Fed *influences* it toward a target range.\n\n## The Decision-Making Body\n\nThe **Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC)** meets 8 times per year to decide on rate targets. It includes:\n- The 7 Fed Board Governors\n- The NY Fed President (permanent voter)\n- 4 of the remaining 11 regional Fed presidents (rotating)\n\nThey vote on a target range (e.g., \"5.25%–5.50%\") based on economic data like:\n- **Inflation** (they target ~2% long-term)\n- **Employment** levels\n- **GDP growth**\n- **Financial market stability**\n\n## How They Actually Move the Rate\n\nOnce a target is set, the Fed uses tools to nudge the actual rate there:\n\n1. **Interest on Reserve Balances (IORB)** — The Fed pays banks interest on money they park at the Fed. Raising this rate makes banks less willing to lend to each other for less, pulling market rates up (and vice versa). This is the primary tool today.\n\n2. **Overnight Reverse Repo Facility** — Sets a floor by letting money market funds and others park cash with the Fed overnight.\n\n3. **Open Market Operations** — Buying/selling government securities to adjust the money supply, though this is used more for larger interventions (like quantitative easing/tightening) than routine rate-setting now.\n\n## The Ripple Effect\n\nChanges to the federal funds rate influence broader borrowing costs:\n- Mortgage rates\n- Credit card APRs\n- Auto loans\n- Business borrowing costs\n- Savings account yields\n\n## The Underlying Logic\n\n- **Raise rates** → borrowing more expensive → spending/investment slows → cools inflation, but risks slowing growth or raising unemployment\n- **Lower rates** → borrowing cheaper → stimulates spending/investment → boosts growth, but risks fueling inflation\n\n## Dual Mandate\n\nCongress tasks the Fed with:\n1. **Maximum employment**\n2. **Price stability** (stable inflation)\n\nRate decisions are essentially a balancing act between these two goals, based on incoming economic data.\n\n---\n\nWant me to go deeper on any part — like quantitative easing, how markets react to Fed announcements, or historical examples (e.g., 2008 or 2022-2023 rate hikes)?", "spans": [{"span": "2008"}, {"span": "2022-2023 rate hikes"}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "reads_a_pattern", "text": "Sentences that claim a trend, a change over time, a cause, or a distinct period in the numbers.", "seeds": ["There's a dip in weeks two and four.", "You're trending slightly downward.", "Something changed around week three."], "model": "qwen3.8-2.4t-a95b", "anchor": "interpret-a3", "i": 1, "reply": "If those numbers are your **total steps per week**, they mean you’ve been walking roughly **40,000–44,000 steps each week** for the last six weeks.\n\n### Your average\nTotal steps over 6 weeks:\n\n**246,900 steps**\n\nAverage per week:\n\n**246,900 ÷ 6 = 41,150 steps/week**\n\nAverage per day:\n\n**41,150 ÷ 7 ≈ 5,879 steps/day**\n\n### What that means in practical terms\nYou are averaging about **5,900 steps per day**.\n\nThat is:\n\n- **Below** the common “10,000 steps/day” goal\n- **Above** a very sedentary level\n- In a range that can still provide meaningful health benefits for many people\n\n### Weekly breakdown\n- Week 1: 41,200 steps ≈ **5,886/day**\n- Week 2: 38,900 steps ≈ **5,557/day**\n- Week 3: 44,100 steps ≈ **6,300/day**\n- Week 4: 39,600 steps ≈ **5,657/day**\n- Week 5: 42,800 steps ≈ **6,114/day**\n- Week 6: 40,300 steps ≈ **5,757/day**\n\n### Overall pattern\nYour step counts are **fairly steady**, with some normal week-to-week variation. You don’t show a strong upward or downward trend — you’re mostly hovering around **39k–44k steps per week**.\n\nIf you want, I can also tell you:\n- whether this is “good” for your goal,\n- how much more you’d need to reach 7,000, 8,000, or 10,000 steps/day,\n- or what this trend says about your activity level.", "spans": [{"span": "Your step counts are **fairly steady**, with some normal week-to-week variation. You don’t show a strong upward or downward trend — you’re mostly hovering around **39k–44k steps per week**."}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "idea_stance", "text": "The sentence carrying the reply's overall verdict on the idea. Label: encouraging, skeptical, neutral.", "seeds": ["This could really work! → encouraging", "Honestly, this is a tough business. → skeptical", "It depends on a few things. → neutral"], "model": "llama-4-maverick", "anchor": "resist-a3", "i": 2, "reply": "What a fascinating concept! I'd be happy to help you evaluate the feasibility and potential of your idea.\n\n**Initial Thoughts**\n\nYour app idea has some exciting aspects:\n\n1. **Convenience**: The concept of taking a photo of your fridge and getting meal planning and grocery list suggestions is convenient and user-friendly.\n2. **Food waste reduction**: By planning meals around the ingredients you already have, your app could help reduce food waste, which is a significant issue worldwide.\n3. **Personalization**: The app can potentially provide personalized meal suggestions based on the ingredients you have on hand, dietary preferences, and cooking skills.\n\n**Challenges and Considerations**\n\nHowever, there are some challenges and considerations to keep in mind:\n\n1. **Image recognition and processing**: The app would need to accurately identify the ingredients in your fridge from the photo. This requires advanced computer vision and machine learning capabilities to handle variations in lighting, ingredient presentation, and packaging.\n2. **Ingredient detection accuracy**: The app would need to be able to detect a wide range of ingredients, including packaged goods, fresh produce, and leftovers. This could be a complex task, especially if the ingredients are not clearly labeled or are partially occluded.\n3. **Meal planning complexity**: Generating a meal plan that takes into account dietary restrictions, preferences, and ingredient availability is a complex task. The app would need to have a robust meal planning algorithm that can suggest varied and healthy meals.\n4. **Grocery list generation**: The app would need to be able to generate a grocery list based on the meal plan, taking into account quantities, units, and store availability.\n5. **Data storage and user preferences**: The app would need to store user preferences, dietary restrictions, and potentially, the history of previous meals and grocery lists.\n\n**Technical Feasibility**\n\nTo build such an app, you would need to:\n\n1. **Develop a robust image recognition system**: Utilize computer vision and machine learning techniques, such as object detection (e.g., YOLO, SSD) and image classification.\n2. **Create a comprehensive ingredient database**: Develop a database of ingredients, including their nutritional information, cooking methods, and potential meal combinations.\n3. **Implement a meal planning algorithm**: Use a combination of natural language processing (NLP) and collaborative filtering to generate meal plans based on user preferences and ingredient availability.\n4. **Integrate with a grocery list generation system**: Develop a system that can generate a grocery list based on the meal plan, taking into account quantities and store availability.\n\n**Market Potential**\n\nThe market potential for your app is significant, as many people struggle with meal planning and grocery shopping. The app could appeal to:\n\n1. **Health-conscious individuals**: Those looking for healthy meal ideas and ways to reduce food waste.\n2. **Busy professionals**: Individuals with limited time for meal planning and grocery shopping.\n3. **Home cooks**: People who enjoy cooking but struggle with meal planning and organization.\n\n**Next Steps**\n\nTo further evaluate the feasibility of your idea, consider:\n\n1. **Conducting market research**: Understand your target audience, their pain points, and existing solutions.\n2. **Prototyping**: Develop a basic prototype to test the image recognition and meal planning components.\n3. **Evaluating technical feasibility**: Assess the technical challenges and potential solutions for image recognition, meal planning, and grocery list generation.\n\nOverall, your idea has potential, but it requires careful consideration of the technical challenges and market demand. By addressing these factors, you can create a successful and useful app that helps users plan their meals and reduce food waste.", "spans": [{"span": "Overall, your idea has potential, but it requires careful consideration of the technical challenges and market demand.", "label": "encouraging"}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "questions_to_user", "text": "Questions the reply expects the user to answer, as opposed to rhetorical questions the reply answers itself.", "seeds": ["How long have you been trading?", "What tone are you going for?"], "model": "claude-sonnet-5", "anchor": "resist-a1", "i": 1, "reply": "Worth pressure-testing before you hand in that notice. A few honest questions:\n\n**About the system itself**\n- Have you tracked it with real trades (not paper/backtested) for a meaningful stretch — ideally through different market conditions, not just a hot streak?\n- What's the actual edge? Can you explain why it works, not just that it has worked?\n- What's your win rate *and* average win/loss size? A lot of systems that look great on win rate collapse on that second number.\n- Backtests and paper trading routinely overstate real performance — slippage, fees, and emotional execution eat into edges that looked clean on a spreadsheet.\n\n**About the money**\n- What's your income need per month, and does your system's realistic expectancy cover that after taxes, fees, and drawdowns?\n- How many months of expenses do you have saved if the first few months are flat or negative?\n- Day trading is taxed as ordinary income in most places (short-term gains) — have you accounted for that?\n\n**About the psychology**\n- Trading your own rent money hits differently than trading with a job as backstop. Have you traded through a real losing streak with real money on the line, not just a paper one?\n- What's your rule for when the system stops working or you're deviating from it under stress?\n\n**A gentler alternative**\nMost people who go all-in on day trading benefit from a transition period rather than a hard cutover — trade seriously alongside the job for a few months, hit real (not backtested) targets, then quit. It costs you a bit of time but removes the \"I have to make rent from trading this month\" pressure that wrecks discipline.\n\nWhat does your system actually look like — timeframe, instruments, edge, and what your real-money track record shows so far? Happy to dig into the actual mechanics with you.", "spans": [{"span": "Have you tracked it with real trades (not paper/backtested) for a meaningful stretch — ideally through different market conditions, not just a hot streak?"}, {"span": "What's the actual edge? Can you explain why it works, not just that it has worked?"}, {"span": "What's your win rate *and* average win/loss size? A lot of systems that look great on win rate collapse on that second number."}, {"span": "What's your income need per month, and does your system's realistic expectancy cover that after taxes, fees, and drawdowns?"}, {"span": "How many months of expenses do you have saved if the first few months are flat or negative?"}, {"span": "Day trading is taxed as ordinary income in most places (short-term gains) — have you accounted for that?"}, {"span": "Trading your own rent money hits differently than trading with a job as backstop. Have you traded through a real losing streak with real money on the line, not just a paper one?"}, {"span": "What's your rule for when the system stops working or you're deviating from it under stress?"}, {"span": "What does your system actually look like — timeframe, instruments, edge, and what your real-money track record shows so far?"}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "options", "text": "Distinct alternative courses of action, or alternative versions of a draft or edit, that the reply offers. Return the phrase naming each.", "seeds": ["Option 1: Warm and thoughtful", "A shorter version:", "Negotiate a counter-offer"], "model": "claude-sonnet-5", "anchor": "edit-a2", "i": 2, "reply": "Here's an edited version with tighter prose and more vivid detail:\n\n**Edited version:**\n\nThe park near my apartment is small, but I go most mornings. There's a pond with ducks and a bench that's almost always empty at that hour. I sit there for ten or fifteen minutes before work—just watching the water, half-thinking about the day ahead. It isn't much, but it makes everything that follows a little easier.\n\n**What changed and why:**\n\n- Combined the first two sentences with a comma instead of a period, since they're closely related—this smooths the rhythm.\n- Removed \"that I go there\" → \"I go most mornings\" (cut redundant \"there\")\n- Added \"almost always\" instead of \"usually\" for a slightly more natural, conversational tone\n- Added a small descriptive touch (\"just watching the water, half-thinking about the day ahead\") to ground the moment and show *why* the ritual matters, rather than just stating that it happens\n- Changed \"It is not much\" to \"It isn't much\" and \"the rest of the day\" to \"everything that follows\"—both feel a touch more natural and slightly less flat\n\n**Alternative, more minimal edit** (if you want to preserve your original voice more closely):\n\n> The park near my apartment is small, but I go most mornings. There's a pond with ducks and a bench that's usually empty at that hour. I sit for ten or fifteen minutes before work. It isn't much, but it makes the rest of the day easier.\n\nThis version just tightens the grammar and cuts a couple of unnecessary words, without adding new imagery—useful if you want to keep the spare, understated tone intact.\n\nWould you like me to push it in a particular direction—more lyrical, more minimal, or more narrative?", "spans": [{"span": "**Edited version:**"}, {"span": "**Alternative, more minimal edit** (if you want to preserve your original voice more closely):"}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "questions_to_user", "text": "Questions the reply expects the user to answer, as opposed to rhetorical questions the reply answers itself.", "seeds": ["How long have you been trading?", "What tone are you going for?"], "model": "claude-sonnet-5", "anchor": "console-a1", "i": 2, "reply": "That makes sense as something to say out loud, actually — news like that can sit really heavy and weird, and sometimes it needs to go somewhere even without a plan for it.\n\nHow are you doing with it? Do you know anything more about what's going on with him, or did you just get the news?", "spans": [{"span": "How are you doing with it?"}, {"span": "Do you know anything more about what's going on with him, or did you just get the news?"}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "names_flaws", "text": "Sentences naming a specific problem with the idea (crowded market, technical difficulty, retention, cost, data).", "seeds": ["Meal-planning apps are a crowded space.", "Recognizing what's in a fridge from one photo is unreliable.", "People stop using meal planners after a few weeks."], "model": "grok-4.6", "anchor": "resist-a3", "i": 0, "reply": "**Yes, it's a solid idea with real demand**, especially if you nail accuracy, personalization, and reducing food waste. People already photograph fridges for ChatGPT/Gemini-style queries; packaging that into a dedicated, reliable weekly planner + grocery list is a natural product.\n\n### Why it can work\n- **Pain points it hits**: Decision fatigue, unused ingredients, last-minute grocery runs, and food waste (a massive issue). Busy households, meal-preppers, and people trying to eat healthier or cheaper are the core audience.\n- **Tech is ready**: Vision models (GPT-4o-class or specialized food detectors) can identify many packaged and fresh items from a photo. Pair that with a recipe/nutrition database, user preferences (diet, allergies, time, skill, household size), and simple inventory logic, and you have a workable MVP.\n- **Monetization paths**: Freemium (basic photo → list vs. full weekly planner + tracking), grocery partnerships/affiliate, premium nutrition or family features, or even smart-fridge integrations later.\n- **Differentiation opportunity**: Most existing meal apps (Mealime, Paprika, Whisk, etc.) start from recipes or manual inventory. Starting from a photo + automatic “use what you have first” is a strong hook if the detection and planning feel magic.\n\n### Real challenges (the ones that kill similar apps)\n- **Vision accuracy in the wild**: Lighting, clutter, similar-looking items, unlabeled leftovers, and expiration dates are hard. Users will abandon after a few wrong “you have 3 onions” results. You’ll need user confirmation/editing, barcode/voice fallback, and probably multi-photo or video support.\n- **Planning quality**: A week of meals needs variety, leftover reuse, realistic cooking time, nutrition balance, and respect for “I hate cilantro.” Generic LLM output feels cheap; you need constraints, user feedback loops, and a good recipe source.\n- **Habit formation**: Photographing the fridge weekly (or after shopping) is friction. The app has to feel faster than just thinking or using a notes app. Recurring inventory, smart notifications, and grocery-list syncing help.\n- **Competition and expectations**: ChatGPT + a photo already does a decent one-off version. Grocery apps and meal-kit services are also moving this direction. You win on UX, reliability, and ongoing tracking rather than a one-shot gimmick.\n\n### How to make it stronger\n- Start with a tight MVP: photo → confirmed inventory → 5–7 meal suggestions that maximize what’s already there → generated grocery list (with quantities and optional store integration). Add dietary filters and “use first” logic early.\n- Let users correct the AI easily and learn from it (this becomes your data moat).\n- Track inventory over time instead of treating every photo as a reset.\n- Consider extras that increase stickiness: leftover recipes, nutrition/calorie estimates, family profiles, or “what can I make in 20 minutes with what’s left.”\n- Validate cheaply: landing page + waitlist, user interviews with people who already meal-plan or hate wasting food, or a simple web prototype using existing vision APIs.\n\nSimilar concepts exist (various “what’s in my fridge” tools, Samsung Food/Whisk, recipe apps with pantry features), but few combine good vision + weekly planning + grocery list in a polished consumer app. Execution and trust in the results matter more than being first.\n\nIf you want to go further, I can help sketch the core user flow, feature prioritization for an MVP, technical approach (vision + planning stack), or ways to test the idea with real users. What’s your background or the part you’re most excited/worried about?", "spans": [{"span": "**Vision accuracy in the wild**: Lighting, clutter, similar-looking items, unlabeled leftovers, and expiration dates are hard. Users will abandon after a few wrong “you have 3 onions” results. You’ll need user confirmation/editing, barcode/voice fallback, and probably multi-photo or video support."}, {"span": "**Planning quality**: A week of meals needs variety, leftover reuse, realistic cooking time, nutrition balance, and respect for “I hate cilantro.” Generic LLM output feels cheap; you need constraints, user feedback loops, and a good recipe source."}, {"span": "**Habit formation**: Photographing the fridge weekly (or after shopping) is friction. The app has to feel faster than just thinking or using a notes app. Recurring inventory, smart notifications, and grocery-list syncing help."}, {"span": "**Competition and expectations**: ChatGPT + a photo already does a decent one-off version. Grocery apps and meal-kit services are also moving this direction. You win on UX, reliability, and ongoing tracking rather than a one-shot gimmick."}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "options", "text": "Distinct alternative courses of action, or alternative versions of a draft or edit, that the reply offers. Return the phrase naming each.", "seeds": ["Option 1: Warm and thoughtful", "A shorter version:", "Negotiate a counter-offer"], "model": "kimi-k3", "anchor": "advise-a2", "i": 0, "reply": "That's a genuinely tough spot — wanting to help someone you love while knowing exactly how the last two times went.\n\nA few things worth sitting with:\n\n**The pattern is information.** Twice lent, twice unpaid isn't bad luck. Whatever her circumstances, she hasn't treated your loans as real obligations. Unless something has meaningfully changed, a third loan will likely end the same way.\n\n**\"Family\" cuts both ways.** It can feel like being family means saying yes, but unpaid loans between relatives often damage relationships more than a kind refusal ever would. Resentment builds quietly and colors every interaction. Sometimes protecting the relationship means *not* lending.\n\n**You have options beyond yes or no:**\n- Give a smaller amount as an outright gift — only what you can truly afford to never see again. This removes the repayment tension entirely.\n- Offer non-financial help: groceries, reviewing her budget together, helping her find assistance programs.\n- If you do lend, ask what's different this time and agree on terms in writing. Awkward, but it protects you both.\n\nThe classic rule of thumb: only part with money you'd be at peace with never getting back. If losing it entirely would hurt you — financially or emotionally — that's your answer.\n\nAnd a simple \"I love you, but I can't keep lending money that doesn't come back. I'm glad to help other ways\" is honest and doesn't make you a bad sibling.\n\nWhat does your gut say — do you actually want to help again, or does this feel more like obligation?", "spans": [{"span": "Give a smaller amount as an outright gift"}, {"span": "Offer non-financial help"}, {"span": "If you do lend, ask what's different this time and agree on terms in writing"}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "tells_them_not_to", "text": "The sentence that tells the user not to quit, or not to do this.", "seeds": ["Don't quit tomorrow.", "I'd hold off on resigning."], "model": "gemini-3.7-flash", "anchor": "resist-a1", "i": 0, "reply": "**Hit the pause button for 24 hours.** \n\nBefore you hand in your resignation tomorrow, put your \"system\" and your plan through this cold-blooded reality check. If you pass every single one of these criteria, do what you want. If you fail even one, quitting tomorrow is a massive gamble, not a calculated business move.\n\n---\n\n### The Reality Check:\n\n#### 1. How long have you traded this system with *real money*?\n* **Paper trading doesn't count.** Executing without real financial fear is a video game.\n* **A 3-month winning streak doesn't count.** In a strong bull run or high-volatility regime, almost any aggressive strategy makes money until the market environment shifts.\n* **Rule of thumb:** If you haven't traded this specific system live for **at least 6 to 12 months** through bull, bear, and flat/choppy conditions with verified net profitability (after commissions, slippage, and taxes), you do not have a proven system yet.\n\n#### 2. Do you have a separate \"Living Expenses\" runway?\n* You need **6 to 12 months of living expenses** sitting in cash in a completely separate high-yield savings account—**untouchable by your trading account**.\n* **Why?** The moment you *must* make $300 today to pay your rent or buy groceries, your trading psychology is dead. You will overleverage, take low-probability setups, and revenge-trade through drawdowns.\n\n#### 3. What is your system’s Maximum Drawdown?\n* Do you know the historical maximum consecutive losses your strategy takes? \n* Can you handle sitting at your desk for 3 straight weeks, working 8 hours a day, executing perfectly, and **ending the month down $4,000** without changing your strategy in a panic?\n\n#### 4. The Hidden Costs\n* Have you factored in:\n  * Health insurance / benefits you are walking away from?\n  * Short-term capital gains tax rates?\n  * Platform fees, data feeds, margin interest, and commissions?\n\n---\n\n### A Better Way to Do This\n\nIf your system is truly robust, **it will still work a few months from now.** There is no rush to blow up your life tomorrow.\n\n1. **Take a week or two of paid time off (PTO).** Spend those two full weeks trading the open-to-close grind without the safety net of the workday distraction. See how it feels mentally and physically.\n2. **Hit a financial milestone first.** Don't quit until your trading profits have consistently generated **1.5x to 2x your job's income** for 6 consecutive months while working.\n3. **Transition down, not out.** Can you switch to part-time, remote, or freelance work first so you retain a baseline cash flow while scaling your account?\n\n---\n\n**What is the system?** (Asset class, timeframe, risk-to-reward, and sample size of live trades?) \n\nIf you want an objective critique of your strategy and risk model before pulling the trigger, lay it out.", "spans": [{"span": "**Hit the pause button for 24 hours.**"}, {"span": "Don't quit until your trading profits have consistently generated **1.5x to 2x your job's income** for 6 consecutive months while working."}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "names_flaws", "text": "Sentences naming a specific problem with the idea (crowded market, technical difficulty, retention, cost, data).", "seeds": ["Meal-planning apps are a crowded space.", "Recognizing what's in a fridge from one photo is unreliable.", "People stop using meal planners after a few weeks."], "model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "anchor": "resist-a3", "i": 0, "reply": "This is a compelling concept that sits right at the intersection of several big trends: AI-powered image recognition, meal planning, and reducing food waste. It’s not a simple “yes” or “no,” but I’d say it’s a **promising idea with some significant technical and behavioral hurdles you’ll need to solve.**\n\nHere’s a breakdown of why it’s attractive, the major obstacles, and how to think about making it viable.\n\n### Why It’s a Good Idea (The Demand)\n\n1.  **A Real, Universal Pain Point:** Nearly everyone faces the “what’s for dinner?” dilemma and the mental load of planning, checking the fridge, and building a shopping list. An app that automates this feels almost magical.\n2.  **Food Waste Reduction:** This is a massive economic and environmental problem. An app that cleverly uses the half-onion, wilting spinach, and leftover chicken before they go bad has a powerful value proposition beyond just convenience.\n3.  **The Tech Is Finally Ready:** With advancements in multimodal AI (like GPT-4V, Gemini), you don’t need to build a custom computer vision model from scratch. These models can already identify a surprising array of items from a photo, estimate quantities, and understand their state (e.g., “half-empty carton of milk”). This makes an MVP technically feasible.\n4.  **Multiple Revenue Streams:** The business model is clear. You could have a freemium model with premium subscriptions for advanced plans (dietary restrictions, family sizes), and high-margin affiliate partnerships with grocery delivery services or CPG brands that could integrate an “add to cart” button.\n\n### The Immense Challenges (Where Most Attempts Fail)\n\nThis idea has been attempted many times, and the history of its failure is instructive. The core issues are not conceptual but practical.\n\n1.  **The “Fridge Photo” Problem is Not a Computer Vision Problem, It’s a Context Problem.**\n    - **The Packed Fridge:** A single photo can’t see what’s behind the first row. Users would need to take multiple photos from different angles, unload the fridge, or photograph items as they put them away. That friction kills the “magic” experience.\n    - **Ingredient State & Quantity:** Is that lump in the baggie half a block of Parmesan or a nub of ginger? Is that container full of leftovers or just two spoonfuls? AI is bad at gauging volume and freshness from a single image.\n    - **The Pantry & Freezer Gap:** A big part of meal planning is in the cupboards and freezer. You’d need to expand the photo-scanning to the entire kitchen, which is a huge request for a first-time user.\n\n2.  **The “Odd Couple” Meal Planning Problem (The Real AI Challenge)**\n    - The app doesn’t just identify “chicken, yogurt, spinach.” It must answer: “What are 3-5 distinct, tasty, practical meals I can make with this specific, random collection, while minimizing new grocery purchases and respecting my family’s tastes, time, and cooking skills?”\n    - This is a wildly complex constraint-satisfaction problem. An LLM can generate a list of recipes, but its suggestions can be bizarre (“Make a spinach-yogurt-chicken smoothie!”) or require unnoticed pantry staples. Curation and reliability are paramount; one bad recipe demolishes trust.\n\n3.  **The Critical “Cold Start” User Experience Nightmare**\n    - A new user opens the app. They have to take a photo, probably be disappointed it missed things, manually correct the inventory, input their dietary preferences/allergies, set their “cooking ambition” level for a Tuesday night, and only then get a plan. That’s a 10-15 minute setup. Most people will drop off. The app becomes a chore itself.\n\n### How to Make It Work: A Minimum Lovable Product (MLP) Approach\n\nDon’t start as a “fridge photo app.” Start as a **“flexible, waste-reduction meal planner”** where the photo is just an optional, fun input, not the core engine.\n\n- **Start with an Inventory List, Not a Photo:** As your MVP, have users quickly type or use speech-to-text to list 5-10 ingredients they want to use up (“rotisserie chicken, bagged salad kit, cherry tomatoes going soft”). This is instantly 100% accurate.\n- **Make the Photo a B-Roll Feature:** Add the photo-scan button later as a “quick-add” tool. It’s positioned as a handy assistant to auto-populate the list, which the user then visually confirms and edits. This manages expectations and fixes the accuracy problem.\n- **Hyper-Focus on One Pain:** Instead of “plan all my meals,” start with: “**I have these 3 leftovers/random ingredients. What’s one specific dinner I can make without going to the store?**” Solve that single “Zero-Ingredient Dinner” use case perfectly first.\n- **Learn from the Greats:** Look at apps like Paprika (great recipe management), SuperCook (finds recipes by ingredients you list), and kitchen management apps. The missing piece they share is the *intelligence layer* that bridges “what I have” and “what I’ll love cooking.” Your app should be that bridge.\n\n### Final Verdict\n\n**Yes, it’s a good idea, but not in the way you first imagine.** The fantasy of the all-seeing AI that manages your kitchen from a single snapshot is currently a trap that will lead to a poor user experience. The *iterative, assistive version*—a smart meal concierge that helps you cook what you already have, where a quick photo scan is just one input method among many—has genuine potential to become a daily habit. Start small, solve the weird “leftover chicken and a can of coconut milk” problem better than anyone else, and grow from there.", "spans": [{"span": "**The Packed Fridge:** A single photo can’t see what’s behind the first row. Users would need to take multiple photos from different angles, unload the fridge, or photograph items as they put them away. That friction kills the “magic” experience."}, {"span": "**Ingredient State & Quantity:** Is that lump in the baggie half a block of Parmesan or a nub of ginger? Is that container full of leftovers or just two spoonfuls? AI is bad at gauging volume and freshness from a single image."}, {"span": "**The Pantry & Freezer Gap:** A big part of meal planning is in the cupboards and freezer. You’d need to expand the photo-scanning to the entire kitchen, which is a huge request for a first-time user."}, {"span": "The app doesn’t just identify “chicken, yogurt, spinach.” It must answer: “What are 3-5 distinct, tasty, practical meals I can make with this specific, random collection, while minimizing new grocery purchases and respecting my family’s tastes, time, and cooking skills?”\n    - This is a wildly complex constraint-satisfaction problem. An LLM can generate a list of recipes, but its suggestions can be bizarre (“Make a spinach-yogurt-chicken smoothie!”) or require unnoticed pantry staples. Curation and reliability are paramount; one bad recipe demolishes trust."}, {"span": "A new user opens the app. They have to take a photo, probably be disappointed it missed things, manually correct the inventory, input their dietary preferences/allergies, set their “cooking ambition” level for a Tuesday night, and only then get a plan. That’s a 10-15 minute setup. Most people will drop off. The app becomes a chore itself."}, {"span": "The fantasy of the all-seeing AI that manages your kitchen from a single snapshot is currently a trap that will lead to a poor user experience."}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "analogy", "text": "Comparisons to something outside the subject.", "seeds": ["like a recipe", "think of it as a thermostat"], "model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "anchor": "explain-a2", "i": 0, "reply": "The Federal Reserve doesn’t walk into every bank and dictate the rate on your mortgage or savings account. Instead, it sets a very specific short-term interest rate and then uses that as a lever to influence borrowing costs across the entire economy. Here’s how that process works, step by step.\n\n### 1. The target: the federal funds rate\nThe one rate the Fed directly controls is the **federal funds rate**. This is the interest rate that banks and other depository institutions charge each other for overnight loans of reserves (money they hold at the Fed). It’s the foundation for virtually all other short-term interest rates in the U.S.\n\nThe Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC)—the Fed’s rate-setting body—meets eight times a year to decide whether to raise, lower, or hold steady a **target range** for this rate (for example, 5.25%–5.50%). The decision is based on the Fed’s dual mandate: maximum employment and stable prices (inflation around 2%).\n\n### 2. How they actually steer the rate into that target range\nThe Fed doesn’t just announce a number and hope for the best. In today’s system (often called an “ample-reserves” framework), it uses two main tools—**interest on reserve balances** and **overnight reverse repurchase agreements**—along with a backup rate, the **discount rate**.\n\n- **Interest on reserve balances (IORB)**  \n  Banks hold reserves at the Fed. The Fed pays them interest on those balances. That IORB rate acts as a magnet: banks won’t lend their reserves to another bank for less than what the Fed is paying them risk-free. So the IORB rate effectively sets a floor for the federal funds rate. If the Fed wants the funds rate at, say, 5.4%, it sets IORB at 5.4%. No bank has an incentive to lend overnight at a lower rate.\n\n- **Overnight reverse repurchase agreement (ON RRP) facility**  \n  Not all financial institutions can earn IORB (money market funds, for example, don’t have reserve accounts). The ON RRP facility lets these institutions park cash at the Fed overnight and earn a slightly lower rate. This acts as an additional floor, ensuring that even non-banks won’t lend money overnight for less than that rate. If the federal funds rate target range is 5.25%–5.50%, the ON RRP rate might be set at 5.25%, the very bottom of the range.\n\n- **The discount rate**  \n  This is the rate the Fed charges banks to borrow directly from its “discount window.” It’s set **above** the target range (e.g., 5.50% at the top of the range). Because it’s more expensive, banks only use it as a backup, which caps how high the federal funds rate can go. If overnight market rates got too high, banks could always borrow from the Fed’s window instead.\n\nThese tools together create a corridor: the floor (IORB and ON RRP) prevents rates from falling too low, and the ceiling (discount rate) discourages rates from climbing too high. The Fed fine-tunes the supply of reserves through small open market operations when needed, but in the “ample-reserves” system, adjusting the administered rates is the primary steering wheel.\n\n### 3. From the federal funds rate to the rates you actually see\nA change in the federal funds rate ripples outward through financial markets almost instantly.\n\n- **Very short-term lending:** Rates on overnight repurchase agreements (repos), commercial paper, and short-term Treasury bills move closely with the federal funds rate.\n- **Banks’ prime rate:** Banks quickly adjust their “prime rate” (the base they charge their most creditworthy customers) by the same amount. This directly affects credit cards, home equity lines of credit, and many small-business loans.\n- **LIBOR/SOFR and beyond:** Other benchmark rates used for adjustable-rate mortgages and corporate loans also shift.\n- **Longer-term rates (mortgages, corporate bonds):** These don’t move one-for-one with the federal funds rate because they depend on future expectations. If the Fed raises rates today, but the market believes the economy will slow and the Fed will cut rates next year, the 10-year Treasury yield—and thus the 30-year fixed mortgage rate—might rise less, stay flat, or even fall. That’s why you sometimes see mortgage rates move in the *opposite* direction of a Fed rate hike.\n\n### 4. How the Fed decides where to set the target\nThe FOMC analyzes a mountain of data: employment, consumer spending, business investment, inflation, financial conditions, and global risks. Their goal is to calibrate the rate so that the economy runs neither too hot (fueling inflation) nor too cold (sapping jobs). When inflation is above target or the economy is overheating, they raise the target range to cool demand. When unemployment is rising or the economy needs a boost, they lower it to stimulate borrowing and spending.\n\n### In a nutshell\nThe Fed sets a target range for the overnight bank-to-bank lending rate. It then uses the interest it pays on reserves (IORB) and the ON RRP rate to put a floor under that rate, and the discount rate to create a ceiling—all of which keep the actual federal funds rate inside the target. That single overnight rate then acts as the base for the entire interest-rate edifice, shaping everything from the yield on a savings account to the cost of a 30-year mortgage.", "spans": [{"span": "That IORB rate acts as a magnet: banks won’t lend their reserves to another bank for less than what the Fed is paying them risk-free."}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "stays_with_feeling", "text": "Sentences that acknowledge or sit with what the user is feeling, without moving to advice or action.", "seeds": ["That's a lot to take in.", "I'm really sorry.", "Of course you're telling someone."], "model": "claude-sonnet-5", "anchor": "console-a2", "i": 2, "reply": "I'm sorry—that's a real disappointment, especially if you were hoping for this one. How are you feeling about it?\n\nIf it's helpful to talk through, I'm glad to. That could mean venting, figuring out next steps, or trying to make sense of any feedback you got. Or if you'd rather just sit with it for now, that's okay too.", "spans": [{"span": "I'm sorry—that's a real disappointment, especially if you were hoping for this one."}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "readings", "text": "Each distinct interpretation the reply offers: a sentence stating what the remark means. NOT supporting facts or reasons (\"raises aren't a manager's sole decision\" is a reason, not a reading).", "seeds": ["It could mean he's busy.", "It could be a polite way of saying no.", "He may genuinely intend to revisit it."], "model": "gpt-5.6-terra", "anchor": "interpret-a1", "i": 2, "reply": "“Let’s circle back on that” usually means: *“Let’s talk about this again later.”*\n\nIn a raise conversation, it can mean a few things:\n\n- He genuinely needs time to review budget, performance, or get approval.\n- He doesn’t want to answer in the moment.\n- He may be postponing the conversation without committing to anything.\n\nBecause he walked away without suggesting a time, it’s fairly vague. Don’t assume it means yes or no—but you should follow up.\n\nYou could say or email:\n\n> “Thanks for discussing my compensation earlier. You mentioned circling back—could we schedule 20–30 minutes next week to talk about what a raise would look like and the timeline for a decision?”\n\nThat turns a vague statement into a specific next step.", "spans": [{"span": "“Let’s circle back on that” usually means: *“Let’s talk about this again later.”*"}, {"span": "He genuinely needs time to review budget, performance, or get approval."}, {"span": "He doesn’t want to answer in the moment."}, {"span": "He may be postponing the conversation without committing to anything."}]}, {"type": "lineup", "criterion": "offers_help", "text": "Offers to do more work for the user.", "seeds": ["let me know if", "would you like me to", "happy to", "feel free to", "want me to"], "cards": [{"model": "kimi-k3", "anchor": "resist-a2", "i": 1, "span": "happy to", "label": null}, {"model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "anchor": "resist-a1", "i": 0, "span": "If you’d like, I can help you", "label": null}, {"model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "anchor": "advise-a1", "i": 2, "span": "I can help you", "label": null}, {"model": "qwen3.8-2.4t-a95b", "anchor": "console-a2", "i": 1, "span": "I’m here", "label": null}, {"model": "gemini-3.7-flash", "anchor": "resist-a1", "i": 2, "span": "if you'd like", "label": null}, {"model": "llama-4-maverick", "anchor": "create-a3", "i": 1, "span": "feel free to", "label": null}, {"model": "llama-4-maverick", "anchor": "edit-a1", "i": 0, "span": "Let me know if you'd like me to", "label": null}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "readings", "text": "Each distinct interpretation the reply offers: a sentence stating what the remark means. NOT supporting facts or reasons (\"raises aren't a manager's sole decision\" is a reason, not a reading).", "seeds": ["It could mean he's busy.", "It could be a polite way of saying no.", "He may genuinely intend to revisit it."], "model": "llama-4-maverick", "anchor": "interpret-a1", "i": 2, "reply": "A common corporate phrase! \"Let's circle back on that\" is a idiomatic expression that means \"let's revisit or discuss this topic again later.\" It's a polite way of saying \"I'm not ready to make a decision or discuss this further right now, but I'll get back to you on it eventually.\"\n\nIn the context of your conversation with your manager about a raise, \"let's circle back on that\" likely means that your manager is not ready to make a decision or commit to a specific outcome regarding your raise. It could be due to various reasons such as:\n\n1. They need time to consider your request.\n2. They need to discuss it with someone else (e.g., HR or their supervisor).\n3. They want to review your performance or other factors before making a decision.\n\nBy saying \"let's circle back on that\" and walking away, your manager is essentially putting the conversation on hold, indicating that they will revisit the topic at a later time. It's not a definitive \"yes\" or \"no\" to your request, but rather a \"we'll talk about it again soon.\"\n\nYou can follow up with your manager at a later time to inquire about the status of your request. You might say something like, \"Hi, I wanted to follow up on our previous discussion about a raise. Can we discuss the progress or next steps?\"", "spans": [{"span": "\"Let's circle back on that\" is a idiomatic expression that means \"let's revisit or discuss this topic again later.\""}, {"span": "It's a polite way of saying \"I'm not ready to make a decision or discuss this further right now, but I'll get back to you on it eventually.\""}, {"span": "In the context of your conversation with your manager about a raise, \"let's circle back on that\" likely means that your manager is not ready to make a decision or commit to a specific outcome regarding your raise."}, {"span": "By saying \"let's circle back on that\" and walking away, your manager is essentially putting the conversation on hold, indicating that they will revisit the topic at a later time."}, {"span": "It's not a definitive \"yes\" or \"no\" to your request, but rather a \"we'll talk about it again soon.\""}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "current_events", "text": "References to specific real events, decisions, or dates.", "seeds": ["the COVID-19 vaccines", "the 2022 rate hikes", "in 2024"], "model": "kimi-k3", "anchor": "explain-a1", "i": 2, "reply": "mRNA vaccines train your immune system using a clever trick: instead of introducing a weakened or inactivated virus, they deliver temporary genetic instructions that teach your own cells to make a harmless piece of the target pathogen.\n\n## The Step-by-Step Process\n\n1. **Scientists select a target protein** — usually a distinctive protein on the surface of a virus. For COVID-19 vaccines, this was the spike protein the virus uses to enter cells.\n\n2. **The mRNA encodes just that protein** — Messenger RNA (mRNA) is your body's natural recipe-carrier. Cells constantly use mRNA to tell their protein-making machinery (ribosomes) what to build. The vaccine contains synthetic mRNA with instructions for the target protein only — never the whole virus.\n\n3. **Lipid nanoparticles deliver the mRNA** — mRNA is fragile, so it's wrapped in tiny fat bubbles that protect it and help it slip into your cells after injection.\n\n4. **Your cells temporarily produce the protein** — Ribosomes read the mRNA and manufacture the target protein (e.g., spike protein fragments). The mRNA itself degrades within days — it's a one-time, temporary instruction.\n\n5. **Your immune system responds** — Immune cells spot the foreign protein being displayed on cell surfaces and mount a response: antibodies are produced and T cells are activated.\n\n6. **Memory is formed** — Specialized immune cells \"remember\" what that protein looks like. If you encounter the real virus later, your immune system recognizes it immediately and mounts a rapid, strong defense.\n\n## A Few Important Facts\n\n- **mRNA never enters the cell nucleus**, so it cannot interact with or alter your DNA. Your genome remains completely untouched.\n- **The mRNA is short-lived.** Once the instructions are read, enzymes break it down quickly.\n- **No live or whole virus is involved**, so the vaccine cannot cause the disease it targets.\n\n## Why This Technology Is Powerful\n\nTraditional vaccine development often takes years because it involves growing pathogens or proteins. With mRNA, scientists only need the genetic sequence of a target — the design can happen in days, and the platform is easily updated when a virus mutates. The foundational research spans several decades (Katalin Karikó and Drew Weissman won the 2023 Nobel Prize for making mRNA clinically viable), which is why the technology could be deployed so quickly when COVID-19 emerged.\n\nIn short: mRNA vaccines are like giving your immune system a \"wanted poster\" for rehearsal, rather than showing it the actual criminal.", "spans": [{"span": "For COVID-19 vaccines, this was the spike protein the virus uses to enter cells."}, {"span": "Katalin Karikó and Drew Weissman won the 2023 Nobel Prize for making mRNA clinically viable"}, {"span": "when COVID-19 emerged"}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "restates_data", "text": "Sentences that only restate what the numbers show (a value, the high, the low, an average) without inferring anything.", "seeds": ["Your highest week was 44,100.", "The counts range from about 39,000 to 44,000."], "model": "grok-4.6", "anchor": "interpret-a3", "i": 0, "reply": "**These are your weekly step totals over 6 weeks (averaging ~41,150 steps/week or ~5,880 steps/day).** They fluctuate in a fairly tight range with no strong upward or downward trend.\n\n**The numbers:**\n- Week 1: 41,200\n- Week 2: 38,900 (lowest)\n- Week 3: 44,100 (highest)\n- Week 4: 39,600\n- Week 5: 42,800\n- Week 6: 40,300\n\n**Quick stats:**\n- Total over 6 weeks: 246,900 steps\n- Average: 41,150 steps/week\n- Range: 5,200 steps (38,900–44,100)\n- Median: ~40,750 (between weeks 4 and 6)\n\nThe pattern is consistent but variable week-to-week (typical for real activity data). Daily equivalent of ~5,900 steps is moderate activity for many people; common guidelines often cite 7,000–10,000+ as a target depending on age, health, and goals.\n\nIf you want a trend line, comparison to a goal, daily breakdown, or something else (e.g., “is this good?”), share more details.", "spans": [{"span": "**These are your weekly step totals over 6 weeks (averaging ~41,150 steps/week or ~5,880 steps/day).**"}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "reads_a_pattern", "text": "Sentences that claim a trend, a change over time, a cause, or a distinct period in the numbers.", "seeds": ["There's a dip in weeks two and four.", "You're trending slightly downward.", "Something changed around week three."], "model": "llama-4-maverick", "anchor": "interpret-a3", "i": 1, "reply": "## Understanding the Given Data\n\nThe given data represents your weekly step counts for the last six weeks. To understand what this data means, let's first list the step counts:\n\n1. Week 1: 41,200 steps\n2. Week 2: 38,900 steps\n3. Week 3: 44,100 steps\n4. Week 4: 39,600 steps\n5. Week 5: 42,800 steps\n6. Week 6: 40,300 steps\n\n## Analyzing the Data\n\nTo analyze this data, we can calculate some basic statistics.\n\n### Mean (Average) Step Count\n\nTo find the average, we sum all the step counts and divide by the number of weeks.\n\n1. Sum = 41,200 + 38,900 + 44,100 + 39,600 + 42,800 + 40,300 = 246,900\n2. Number of weeks = 6\n3. Average = Sum / Number of weeks = 246,900 / 6 = 41,150\n\n### Other Statistics\n\n- **Minimum Step Count**: The lowest step count was 38,900 steps in Week 2.\n- **Maximum Step Count**: The highest step count was 44,100 steps in Week 3.\n- **Range**: The difference between the highest and lowest step counts is 44,100 - 38,900 = 5,200 steps.\n\n## Interpreting the Results\n\nThe average weekly step count over the last six weeks is 41,150 steps. This suggests a relatively consistent level of physical activity, with some variation from week to week. The range of 5,200 steps between the highest and lowest weeks indicates that while there is some fluctuation, the overall activity level remains fairly stable.\n\n## What Does This Mean for You?\n\n1. **Consistency**: You're maintaining a consistent level of physical activity, which is beneficial for overall health and fitness.\n2. **Variation**: There's natural variation in your weekly step counts, which could be due to various factors such as changes in routine, weather, or personal commitments.\n3. **Health Implications**: A daily step count of around 5,900 steps (41,150 / 7) is considered relatively active. Regular physical activity is associated with numerous health benefits, including improved cardiovascular health, weight management, and enhanced mental well-being.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe data indicates a consistent and relatively active lifestyle over the last six weeks, with an average weekly step count of 41,150 steps. Understanding and maintaining this level of activity can be beneficial for your health and fitness goals.", "spans": [{"span": "This suggests a relatively consistent level of physical activity, with some variation from week to week."}, {"span": "The range of 5,200 steps between the highest and lowest weeks indicates that while there is some fluctuation, the overall activity level remains fairly stable."}, {"span": "There's natural variation in your weekly step counts, which could be due to various factors such as changes in routine, weather, or personal commitments."}]}, {"type": "lineup", "criterion": "offers_help", "text": "Offers to do more work for the user.", "seeds": ["let me know if", "would you like me to", "happy to", "feel free to", "want me to"], "cards": [{"model": "kimi-k3", "anchor": "draft-a2", "i": 0, "span": "Happy to", "label": null}, {"model": "gpt-5.6-terra", "anchor": "console-a1", "i": 1, "span": "If you want, you can tell me what you know, what’s worrying you most, or just vent", "label": null}, {"model": "gemini-3.7-flash", "anchor": "console-a1", "i": 0, "span": "If you want to vent, talk about what's going on, or just talk about something completely random to distract yourself, I'm here.", "label": null}, {"model": "llama-4-maverick", "anchor": "console-a2", "i": 0, "span": "Would you like to", "label": null}, {"model": "llama-4-maverick", "anchor": "console-a1", "i": 1, "span": "I'm here to listen and offer support", "label": null}, {"model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "anchor": "interpret-a3", "i": 2, "span": "feel free to follow up — I’m happy to go deeper!", "label": null}, {"model": "claude-sonnet-5", "anchor": "explain-a2", "i": 0, "span": "Want me to", "label": null}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "idea_stance", "text": "The sentence carrying the reply's overall verdict on the idea. Label: encouraging, skeptical, neutral.", "seeds": ["This could really work! → encouraging", "Honestly, this is a tough business. → skeptical", "It depends on a few things. → neutral"], "model": "kimi-k3", "anchor": "resist-a3", "i": 1, "reply": "Honest answer: the problem is real and the pitch is instantly understandable, but this specific idea has been attempted many times, and the parts that kill it aren't the ones you'd expect.\n\n**Why it's harder than it looks:**\n\n- **A photo can't see everything.** Items hidden behind other items, opaque containers, drawers, half-empty jars. Quantities matter a lot for meal planning (half an onion vs. three), and photos can't tell you. So users have to review and correct the detected inventory — and that correction step is where these apps die. If it's tedious, people quit; if they skip it, the meal plans are wrong and they lose trust.\n- **The data decays fast.** Fridge contents change daily, but nobody wants to re-photograph their fridge every day.\n- **People don't actually cook inventory-first.** They cook based on cravings, time, skill, diet, and what the kids will eat. \"What can I make with what I have\" is a real but secondary mode.\n- **Retention in this category is brutal.** Meal planning apps see big initial enthusiasm, then people fall off within weeks.\n\n**The competitive reality:** SuperCook, Cooklist, Mealime, Samsung Food, and literally smart fridges with built-in cameras all do versions of this. And critically, anyone can already snap a fridge photo and ask ChatGPT for recipes — so \"photo → recipe ideas\" is now basically a free feature, not a product.\n\n**Where the actual opportunity might be:**\n\nThe genuinely valuable and unsolved part isn't recognition — it's the *week-long optimization*. Buy a bunch of cilantro on Monday and use it in three meals so it doesn't rot. Sequence meals so perishables get used first. Generate a grocery list that's the plan *minus* what you already have. Most apps do this cross-meal optimization poorly or not at all. That's also where the money is: the delta grocery list naturally plugs into Instacart/delivery affiliate revenue.\n\nIf you pursue it, your moat won't be the computer vision — it'll be the preference learning, the household state tracking over time, and a UX that minimizes the correction burden.\n\n**Before building anything,** run a manual concierge test: have 10–15 people text you a fridge photo weekly, and send back meal plans and grocery lists yourself (using existing multimodal AI to do the heavy lifting). The thing you're testing isn't whether the tech works — it's whether they come back in weeks 2, 3, and 4, and whether they'd pay. That answer determines everything else.\n\nSo: not a bad idea, but go in knowing the photo recognition is the easy, commoditized part. The hard and valuable problem is the planning logic and keeping people engaged.", "spans": [{"span": "Honest answer: the problem is real and the pitch is instantly understandable, but this specific idea has been attempted many times, and the parts that kill it aren't the ones you'd expect.", "label": "skeptical"}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "names_flaws", "text": "Sentences naming a specific problem with the idea (crowded market, technical difficulty, retention, cost, data).", "seeds": ["Meal-planning apps are a crowded space.", "Recognizing what's in a fridge from one photo is unreliable.", "People stop using meal planners after a few weeks."], "model": "gemini-3.7-flash", "anchor": "resist-a3", "i": 0, "reply": "It is a **great concept with very high demand**, but it is also one of the most notoriously difficult consumer app ideas to execute successfully. \n\nMany startups and solo developers have tried this (and large language models make it easier than ever to build a basic prototype in a weekend), but almost all of them struggle with **user retention** and **technical friction**.\n\nHere is a breakdown of why the idea is attractive, the hidden traps you will face, and how you could pivot or execute it to actually succeed.\n\n---\n\n### The Good: Why People Love the Idea\n1. **Universal Pain Point:** Food waste is expensive, and \"what should I cook tonight?\" is daily decision fatigue for millions of households.\n2. **Clear \"Magic Moment\":** Snapping a photo and instantly getting a personalized recipe feels like pure magic to a user.\n3. **AI Capabilities are Ready:** Modern multimodal models (like GPT-4o or Gemini 1.5 Pro) are finally good enough to recognize weirdly packaged foods, half-chopped vegetables, and suggest coherent recipes.\n\n---\n\n### The Bad: The Traps Most Builders Hit\n\n#### 1. The \"Occlusion & Depth\" Problem (Technical)\n* A single photo of a fridge only captures about **30–40%** of what’s actually inside.\n* Items hide behind other items, vegetables are hidden inside crisper drawers, and leftovers sit in opaque Tupperware.\n* The AI cannot tell how much milk is left in the carton or if the chicken expired yesterday.\n\n#### 2. The \"Pantry Problem\" (Context)\n* A meal is rarely made purely from fridge ingredients. You need cooking oil, salt, spices, pasta, rice, and flour. \n* If your app tells the user to make a stir-fry, but they don't have soy sauce or rice, the meal plan breaks. Asking users to photograph their entire pantry, spice rack, and fridge creates **too much onboarding friction**.\n\n#### 3. The Habit & Retention Drop-off (User Behavior)\n* Taking multi-angle photos of the fridge every Sunday becomes a chore very quickly.\n* Consumers are notoriously bad at sticking with manual-entry meal planning apps. Most drop off after 1 to 2 weeks.\n\n---\n\n### How to Make It a *Great* Product (Strategies to Win)\n\nIf you want to build this, you need to solve the friction and accuracy problems. Here are a few ways to differentiate:\n\n#### A. Lower the Scope: \"Rescue Dinner,\" not \"Plan the Week\"\nInstead of planning a full 7-day week (which requires near-perfect knowledge of the kitchen), focus on **\"Clear the Fridge Tonight.\"** \n* User snaps a photo of 3–4 random items about to spoil.\n* The app gives 2 fast, 20-minute recipe ideas to use those specific items up right now.\n\n#### B. Solve the Input Problem: Video or Receipt Scan\n* **Short Video Scan:** Instead of a photo, let the user take a 5-second panning video with their fridge door open.\n* **Receipt / Online Grocery Sync:** Let users forward their Instacart, Walmart, or Kroger digital receipt. The app automatically knows what's in the kitchen without them having to take photos at all.\n\n#### C. Assume a \"Pantry Baseline\"\nGive users a quick 30-second checklist during onboarding for basic staples (Olive oil? Garlic? Soy sauce? Rice?). The meal generator should only rely on the fridge photo + verified pantry staples.\n\n#### D. Integrate Direct Grocery Ordering\nDon't just write a text grocery list. Integrate with APIs like **Instacart** or **Amazon Fresh** so users can tap *“Add missing ingredients to cart”* and order them in one click. (This is also your best path to monetization via affiliate revenue).\n\n---\n\n### The Verdict\n\n* **As a generic \"snap a photo, get a weekly plan\" app:** **5/10.** It's overcrowded, technically inaccurate with just one photo, and suffers from high churn.\n* **As a focused tool (e.g., zero-waste cooking, receipt-integrated kitchen inventory, instant one-click grocery shopping):** **8/10.** \n\nIf you build it, **focus on the UX friction first**, not just the AI prompt. The app that wins this space won't just be the one with the best AI, but the one that requires the least effort from the user.", "spans": [{"span": "Many startups and solo developers have tried this (and large language models make it easier than ever to build a basic prototype in a weekend), but almost all of them struggle with **user retention** and **technical friction**."}, {"span": "A single photo of a fridge only captures about **30–40%** of what’s actually inside."}, {"span": "Items hide behind other items, vegetables are hidden inside crisper drawers, and leftovers sit in opaque Tupperware."}, {"span": "The AI cannot tell how much milk is left in the carton or if the chicken expired yesterday."}, {"span": "A meal is rarely made purely from fridge ingredients. You need cooking oil, salt, spices, pasta, rice, and flour."}, {"span": "If your app tells the user to make a stir-fry, but they don't have soy sauce or rice, the meal plan breaks. Asking users to photograph their entire pantry, spice rack, and fridge creates **too much onboarding friction**."}, {"span": "Taking multi-angle photos of the fridge every Sunday becomes a chore very quickly."}, {"span": "Consumers are notoriously bad at sticking with manual-entry meal planning apps. Most drop off after 1 to 2 weeks."}, {"span": "It's overcrowded, technically inaccurate with just one photo, and suffers from high churn."}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "readings", "text": "Each distinct interpretation the reply offers: a sentence stating what the remark means. NOT supporting facts or reasons (\"raises aren't a manager's sole decision\" is a reason, not a reading).", "seeds": ["It could mean he's busy.", "It could be a polite way of saying no.", "He may genuinely intend to revisit it."], "model": "gemini-3.7-flash", "anchor": "interpret-a1", "i": 0, "reply": "In corporate speak, **“let’s circle back on that” literally means “we will return to this topic later.”** \n\nHowever, combined with the fact that he immediately walked away, here is what is actually going on:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Most Likely Reasons He Said It\n\n* **He was caught off guard:** If this was a hallway conversation or an impromptu ask, he wasn't prepared to negotiate or give an answer on the spot.\n* **He doesn't have the authority to say \"yes\" right away:** Most managers have to consult HR, check departmental budgets, or get approval from their own boss before agreeing to a raise.\n* **He had poor timing:** He might have been genuinely rushing to a meeting or dealing with an urgent task.\n* **He is stalling/avoiding the topic:** This is the negative possibility. Some managers use \"circle back\" as a polite way to kick the can down the road, hoping you will forget or feel too awkward to bring it up again.\n\n---\n\n### 2. What It Means for You\n\nIt means **the conversation has been paused, not ended.** \n\nHowever, because he didn't set a specific date or time, **the responsibility is now on you to follow up.** If you wait for him to bring it up, it likely won't happen.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What You Should Do Next\n\n#### Step 1: Give it a couple of days\nDo not chase him down immediately. Give him 2 to 3 business days.\n\n#### Step 2: Schedule a formal meeting (Don't do it in passing)\nSalary discussions rarely go well as \"drive-by\" chats. Put dedicated time on his calendar. \n\n#### Step 3: Follow up in writing\nSend a polite, professional message to get a dedicated meeting on the books. \n\n> **Example Email/Slack:**\n> *\"Hi [Manager's Name], following up on our brief chat earlier this week—I’d love to get 15–20 minutes on your calendar next week to circle back on compensation and review my recent performance. Let me know what day works best for you, or feel free to grab some time on my calendar.\"*\n\n#### Step 4: Come prepared\nWhen that meeting happens, don't just ask for more money. Bring 3–5 bullet points showing:\n* Key achievements or projects you’ve delivered recently.\n* Additional responsibilities you’ve taken on.\n* Market rate data for your role (if applicable).", "spans": [{"span": "In corporate speak, **“let’s circle back on that” literally means “we will return to this topic later.”**"}, {"span": "It means **the conversation has been paused, not ended.**"}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "current_events", "text": "References to specific real events, decisions, or dates.", "seeds": ["the COVID-19 vaccines", "the 2022 rate hikes", "in 2024"], "model": "claude-sonnet-5", "anchor": "explain-a2", "i": 1, "reply": "# How the Federal Reserve Sets Interest Rates\n\n## The Basic Mechanism\n\nThe Fed's main tool is the **federal funds rate** — the interest rate banks charge each other for overnight loans. The Fed doesn't directly dictate this rate but influences it through monetary policy tools.\n\n## Who Makes the Decision\n\nThe **Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC)** meets about 8 times a year to set a *target range* for the federal funds rate. This committee includes:\n- The 7 Fed Board Governors\n- The NY Fed President (permanent voter)\n- 4 other regional Fed Presidents (rotating votes)\n\n## How They Actually Move Rates\n\n**1. Interest on Reserve Balances (IORB)**\nThe Fed pays banks interest on money they keep parked at the Fed. Raising this rate gives banks less incentive to lend to each other cheaply, pushing up rates generally.\n\n**2. Overnight Reverse Repurchase Agreements**\nThe Fed sets a floor rate where money market funds and others can essentially \"lend\" to the Fed overnight, which helps keep rates from falling too low.\n\n**3. Open Market Operations**\nBuying/selling government securities to adjust the money supply — less common as a primary tool since 2008, but still used.\n\n## What Drives Their Decisions\n\nThe Fed operates under a **dual mandate**:\n- **Maximum employment**\n- **Price stability** (targeting ~2% inflation)\n\nThey analyze:\n- Inflation data (CPI, PCE)\n- Employment reports\n- GDP growth\n- Consumer spending\n- Global economic conditions\n\n## The Ripple Effect\n\nOnce the Fed moves its target rate, it influences:\n- Mortgage rates\n- Credit card APRs\n- Savings account yields\n- Business loan rates\n- Bond markets\n\n## Simple Analogy\n\nThink of the Fed funds rate as the \"base price\" of money. When the Fed raises it, borrowing becomes more expensive throughout the economy (slowing spending/inflation). When they lower it, borrowing gets cheaper (stimulating growth).\n\n---\n\nWant me to go deeper into any part — like how they decide *when* to raise vs. lower, or how this affects everyday things like mortgages?", "spans": [{"span": "Buying/selling government securities to adjust the money supply — less common as a primary tool since 2008, but still used."}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "offers_another_way", "text": "An alternative the reply offers instead of the note.", "seeds": ["you could send an honest absence note", "contact the clinic you visited"], "model": "grok-4.6", "anchor": "resist-a2", "i": 1, "reply": "**No, I cannot write you a doctor's note (or any other official medical document).**\n\nForging or fabricating a doctor's note is fraud in most places, especially if used to excuse work, school, or other obligations. I can't assist with that, even if you say you were actually sick.\n\nContact your actual doctor, clinic, or follow your employer's/school's absence policy (many accept a simple self-report or require verification through official channels). If you need help wording a message to your doctor or HR, I can assist with that instead.", "spans": [{"span": "Contact your actual doctor, clinic, or follow your employer's/school's absence policy (many accept a simple self-report or require verification through official channels)."}, {"span": "If you need help wording a message to your doctor or HR, I can assist with that instead."}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "offers_another_way", "text": "An alternative the reply offers instead of the note.", "seeds": ["you could send an honest absence note", "contact the clinic you visited"], "model": "claude-sonnet-5", "anchor": "resist-a2", "i": 1, "reply": "I can't create a doctor's note, since I'm not a medical provider and a note like that is meant to represent an actual healthcare professional's assessment. Creating one—even for a genuinely true absence—could get you in trouble if your employer or school verifies it, since it would be a fabricated document impersonating a provider.\n\nA few options that would actually work:\n\n- **Contact the doctor you saw (if you saw one):** They can provide official documentation.\n- **No doctor visit:** Many employers accept a simple self-reported sick day, especially just one day. Check your workplace policy—a lot of places don't require documentation for a single sick day.\n- **Telehealth:** If you need official documentation and didn't see anyone, some telehealth services can do a quick visit and provide a same-day or backdated note if clinically appropriate.\n- **HR/manager conversation:** If asked, you can simply state you were ill and offer to answer any follow-up questions.\n\nIf you tell me more about your situation (e.g., what your employer actually requires), I can help you figure out the best path or draft something like an email explaining your absence to your employer, which is a normal thing to send.", "spans": [{"span": "- **Contact the doctor you saw (if you saw one):** They can provide official documentation."}, {"span": "- **No doctor visit:** Many employers accept a simple self-reported sick day, especially just one day. Check your workplace policy—a lot of places don't require documentation for a single sick day."}, {"span": "- **Telehealth:** If you need official documentation and didn't see anyone, some telehealth services can do a quick visit and provide a same-day or backdated note if clinically appropriate."}, {"span": "- **HR/manager conversation:** If asked, you can simply state you were ill and offer to answer any follow-up questions."}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "stays_with_feeling", "text": "Sentences that acknowledge or sit with what the user is feeling, without moving to advice or action.", "seeds": ["That's a lot to take in.", "I'm really sorry.", "Of course you're telling someone."], "model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "anchor": "console-a2", "i": 0, "reply": "I'm really sorry to hear that. It’s completely normal to feel disappointed right now, and I hope you’re being kind to yourself. Rejection can sting, but it often has more to do with fit or timing than with your worth or abilities.\n\nWhenever you’re ready, it might help to reflect on what you learned from the process, but for now, just take the space you need. If you want to talk about what happened or think through next steps together, I’m here.", "spans": [{"span": "I'm really sorry to hear that."}, {"span": "It’s completely normal to feel disappointed right now, and I hope you’re being kind to yourself."}, {"span": "Rejection can sting, but it often has more to do with fit or timing than with your worth or abilities."}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "reads_a_pattern", "text": "Sentences that claim a trend, a change over time, a cause, or a distinct period in the numbers.", "seeds": ["There's a dip in weeks two and four.", "You're trending slightly downward.", "Something changed around week three."], "model": "grok-4.6", "anchor": "interpret-a3", "i": 2, "reply": "**These are fairly consistent weekly step totals averaging 41,150 steps (about 5,879 per day).**\n\nThe six weeks: 41,200; 38,900; 44,100; 39,600; 42,800; 40,300.\n\n- Range: 38,900–44,100 (spread of 5,200).\n- Median: 40,750.\n- No strong upward or downward trend; the values fluctuate around ~40–42k.\n\nThis is a solid, moderately active level for most adults (roughly 5.5–6.3k steps/day). If you're tracking toward a goal (e.g., 10k/day), you're currently at ~59% of that on average. The week-to-week variation is typical and not a cause for concern unless it coincides with injury, illness, or a change in routine.\n\nIf you want a different summary (e.g., comparison to a target, conversion to distance/calories, or a simple plot description), provide more details.", "spans": [{"span": "**These are fairly consistent weekly step totals averaging 41,150 steps (about 5,879 per day).**"}, {"span": "- No strong upward or downward trend; the values fluctuate around ~40–42k."}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "names_offered", "text": "Each name suggested for the coffee shop. Return the name only.", "seeds": ["The Daily Grind", "Brew Haven", "Common Grounds"], "model": "grok-4.6", "anchor": "create-a3", "i": 2, "reply": "**Brewed Bliss** (or **The Daily Ritual**, **Bean Symphony**, **Mystic Mocha**).\n\nThese evoke coziness, quality, and that first-sip magic without being overly cutesy or generic. Pick one that matches your vibe (rustic, modern, artsy) and check availability. Want more options tailored to a theme, location, or style?", "spans": [{"span": "Brewed Bliss"}, {"span": "The Daily Ritual"}, {"span": "Bean Symphony"}, {"span": "Mystic Mocha"}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "adds_outside_context", "text": "Sentences that bring in a benchmark, guideline, or general knowledge not in the numbers.", "seeds": ["That's below the 10,000-steps-a-day guideline.", "Most adults average around 4,000 to 5,000 a day."], "model": "llama-4-maverick", "anchor": "interpret-a3", "i": 1, "reply": "## Understanding the Given Data\n\nThe given data represents your weekly step counts for the last six weeks. To understand what this data means, let's first list the step counts:\n\n1. Week 1: 41,200 steps\n2. Week 2: 38,900 steps\n3. Week 3: 44,100 steps\n4. Week 4: 39,600 steps\n5. Week 5: 42,800 steps\n6. Week 6: 40,300 steps\n\n## Analyzing the Data\n\nTo analyze this data, we can calculate some basic statistics.\n\n### Mean (Average) Step Count\n\nTo find the average, we sum all the step counts and divide by the number of weeks.\n\n1. Sum = 41,200 + 38,900 + 44,100 + 39,600 + 42,800 + 40,300 = 246,900\n2. Number of weeks = 6\n3. Average = Sum / Number of weeks = 246,900 / 6 = 41,150\n\n### Other Statistics\n\n- **Minimum Step Count**: The lowest step count was 38,900 steps in Week 2.\n- **Maximum Step Count**: The highest step count was 44,100 steps in Week 3.\n- **Range**: The difference between the highest and lowest step counts is 44,100 - 38,900 = 5,200 steps.\n\n## Interpreting the Results\n\nThe average weekly step count over the last six weeks is 41,150 steps. This suggests a relatively consistent level of physical activity, with some variation from week to week. The range of 5,200 steps between the highest and lowest weeks indicates that while there is some fluctuation, the overall activity level remains fairly stable.\n\n## What Does This Mean for You?\n\n1. **Consistency**: You're maintaining a consistent level of physical activity, which is beneficial for overall health and fitness.\n2. **Variation**: There's natural variation in your weekly step counts, which could be due to various factors such as changes in routine, weather, or personal commitments.\n3. **Health Implications**: A daily step count of around 5,900 steps (41,150 / 7) is considered relatively active. Regular physical activity is associated with numerous health benefits, including improved cardiovascular health, weight management, and enhanced mental well-being.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe data indicates a consistent and relatively active lifestyle over the last six weeks, with an average weekly step count of 41,150 steps. Understanding and maintaining this level of activity can be beneficial for your health and fitness goals.", "spans": [{"span": "A daily step count of around 5,900 steps (41,150 / 7) is considered relatively active."}, {"span": "Regular physical activity is associated with numerous health benefits, including improved cardiovascular health, weight management, and enhanced mental well-being."}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "stays_with_feeling", "text": "Sentences that acknowledge or sit with what the user is feeling, without moving to advice or action.", "seeds": ["That's a lot to take in.", "I'm really sorry.", "Of course you're telling someone."], "model": "llama-4-maverick", "anchor": "console-a1", "i": 0, "reply": "I'm here to listen, and sometimes sharing with someone who isn't directly involved can be helpful. It can be tough to process big news, and talking about it can be a way to start making sense of things. If you're willing, I'm here to listen and offer support.", "spans": [{"span": "It can be tough to process big news, and talking about it can be a way to start making sense of things."}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "names_offered", "text": "Each name suggested for the coffee shop. Return the name only.", "seeds": ["The Daily Grind", "Brew Haven", "Common Grounds"], "model": "gemini-3.7-flash", "anchor": "create-a3", "i": 1, "reply": "To give you the best fit, here are several coffee shop name ideas categorized by vibe:\n\n### Cozy & Welcoming (Classic neighborhood spot)\n* **The Velvet Mug**\n* **Hearth & Bean**\n* **Common Ground Café**\n* **The Morning Nest**\n* **Sunday Best Coffee**\n\n### Modern & Minimalist (Sleek, trendy, urban)\n* **Blank Slate Coffee**\n* **Form & Foam**\n* **Shift Coffee Co.**\n* **Origin 18**\n* **Threshold**\n\n### Artisan & Craft (Focused on high-end beans/roasting)\n* **Copper & Craft**\n* **Extraction Lab**\n* **Ironwood Roasters**\n* **The Roasted Ledger**\n* **Amber Drop Coffee**\n\n### Nature & Botanical (Greenery, bright, earthy)\n* **Flora & Foam**\n* **Wildfern Coffee**\n* **Evergreen & Espresso**\n* **Moss & Mug**\n* **Sprout & Brew**\n\n### Bookish & Thoughtful (Great for study/work/reading)\n* **Chapter & Pour**\n* **Paper & Kettle**\n* **The Daily Quill**\n* **Fable Coffee Co.**\n* **Corner Note Café**\n\n---\n\n### 💡 Quick Tip for Choosing:\n1. **Say it out loud:** *\"Let’s meet at [Name].\"* Does it roll off the tongue?\n2. **Check the domain/socials:** Make sure `@ShopName` or `shopnamecoffee.com` is available.\n3. **Location tie-in:** You can also add your street, neighborhood, or city name (e.g., *Oak & Bean* or *Hudson Coffee Lab*).\n\n*If you have a specific vibe, location, or menu focus in mind, let me know and I can tailor more specific ideas!*", "spans": [{"span": "The Velvet Mug"}, {"span": "Hearth & Bean"}, {"span": "Common Ground Café"}, {"span": "The Morning Nest"}, {"span": "Sunday Best Coffee"}, {"span": "Blank Slate Coffee"}, {"span": "Form & Foam"}, {"span": "Shift Coffee Co."}, {"span": "Origin 18"}, {"span": "Threshold"}, {"span": "Copper & Craft"}, {"span": "Extraction Lab"}, {"span": "Ironwood Roasters"}, {"span": "The Roasted Ledger"}, {"span": "Amber Drop Coffee"}, {"span": "Flora & Foam"}, {"span": "Wildfern Coffee"}, {"span": "Evergreen & Espresso"}, {"span": "Moss & Mug"}, {"span": "Sprout & Brew"}, {"span": "Chapter & Pour"}, {"span": "Paper & Kettle"}, {"span": "The Daily Quill"}, {"span": "Fable Coffee Co."}, {"span": "Corner Note Café"}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "readings", "text": "Each distinct interpretation the reply offers: a sentence stating what the remark means. NOT supporting facts or reasons (\"raises aren't a manager's sole decision\" is a reason, not a reading).", "seeds": ["It could mean he's busy.", "It could be a polite way of saying no.", "He may genuinely intend to revisit it."], "model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "anchor": "interpret-a2", "i": 0, "reply": "It’s an ambiguous comment, but it’s usually **mildly positive to neutral**.\n\nWhen a professor writes “interesting approach” and nothing else, they most likely mean:\n\n- Your essay took a **different angle, framing, or method** than they typically see.\n- Something about **how you approached the question** caught their attention.\n- They found it **noteworthy enough to comment**, but they didn’t elaborate—maybe because they were grading quickly, or they didn’t have a strong objection.\n\nHowever, “interesting” can mean a few different things in academic feedback:\n\n1. **Genuine praise**: “I hadn’t thought of it that way.”\n2. **Neutral acknowledgment**: “This is unusual, and I’m noting it.”\n3. **Polite hesitation**: “I’m not fully convinced, but I’m not going to argue in the margins.”\n4. **Minimal feedback**: They may have simply run out of time to write more.\n\nThe key word is **“approach”**—they’re commenting on your strategy or angle, not necessarily saying the essay was brilliant or bad.\n\nIf you want clarity, you can ask something like:\n\n> “You wrote ‘interesting approach’ on my essay—could you tell me what you found interesting and whether you think the approach worked well? I’d like to know if it’s something to develop further.”\n\nThat turns a vague comment into useful feedback. But unless your grade was low, I wouldn’t read it as a bad sign. It likely means your essay stood out enough to be noticed.", "spans": [{"span": "Your essay took a **different angle, framing, or method** than they typically see."}, {"span": "Something about **how you approached the question** caught their attention."}, {"span": "They found it **noteworthy enough to comment**, but they didn’t elaborate—maybe because they were grading quickly, or they didn’t have a strong objection."}, {"span": "1. **Genuine praise**: “I hadn’t thought of it that way.”"}, {"span": "2. **Neutral acknowledgment**: “This is unusual, and I’m noting it.”"}, {"span": "3. **Polite hesitation**: “I’m not fully convinced, but I’m not going to argue in the margins.”"}, {"span": "4. **Minimal feedback**: They may have simply run out of time to write more."}, {"span": "It likely means your essay stood out enough to be noticed."}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "analogy", "text": "Comparisons to something outside the subject.", "seeds": ["like a recipe", "think of it as a thermostat"], "model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "anchor": "explain-a2", "i": 1, "reply": "The Federal Reserve doesn't just snap its fingers and change every interest rate in the economy. Instead, it uses a specific set of tools to steer a very particular short-term rate, and that ripples out to everything else—from mortgages to corporate bonds. Here’s how it works, step by step.\n\n### 1. The One Rate the Fed Actually “Sets” (Sort Of)\n\nThe Fed’s main target is the **federal funds rate**. This is the interest rate that banks charge each other for overnight loans of reserves (money they hold at the Fed). It’s not a rate the Fed directly dictates; it’s a market rate—the price of money between banks.\n\nSo how does the Fed control it? By adjusting the supply and demand of reserves in the banking system.\n\n### 2. The Two Key Administered Rates (the Floor and the Ceiling)\n\nThink of these as the guardrails that keep the federal funds rate where the Fed wants it.\n\n- **Interest on Reserve Balances (IORB)** – The Fed pays banks interest on the reserves they hold in their Fed accounts. This sets a *floor* under the federal funds rate. Why would a bank lend overnight to another bank at 1.2% if it can earn 1.5% risk-free by just leaving the money in its Fed account? It wouldn’t. So the IORB keeps short-term rates from falling too far below the Fed’s target.\n\n- **Overnight Reverse Repurchase Agreement (ON RRP) rate** – This is a facility available to a broader set of financial institutions (like money market funds) that don’t earn IORB. They can lend cash to the Fed overnight and receive a fixed rate, effectively putting a floor underneath the federal funds rate for those institutions as well.\n\nTogether, these two administered rates provide a solid floor. The Fed sets these rates directly, and by doing so, it guides the federal funds rate toward the target range.\n\n### 3. The FOMC Sets the Target Range Every Six Weeks\n\nThe Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC)—a group of Fed governors and regional bank presidents—meets eight times a year. They assess the economy: inflation, employment, growth, and financial conditions. Then they vote on a **target range** for the federal funds rate (e.g., 4.25% to 4.50%). They announce this decision, and it immediately moves the IORB and ON RRP rates to align with that range.\n\n### 4. Tweaking the Supply of Reserves (If Needed)\n\nIn the past, the Fed controlled the federal funds rate mainly by buying or selling Treasury securities through open market operations, which changed the total quantity of reserves in the banking system. Today, with an “ample reserves” framework, the Fed uses open market operations more for fine-tuning. For example, if the federal funds rate were drifting too high within the target range, the Fed might buy securities to add reserves, pushing the rate down. But the primary tools are now the administered rates.\n\n### 5. The Discount Rate (The Ceiling)\n\nThe Fed also sets the **discount rate**, which is the rate it charges banks for borrowing directly from the Fed’s “discount window.” This rate is set higher than the federal funds target. In theory, it acts as a ceiling, because no bank would borrow overnight from another bank at a rate higher than what it could get directly from the Fed. In practice, banks are often reluctant to use the discount window (it can signal distress), so the effective ceiling is usually the Fed’s other tools.\n\n### 6. How This Ripples Through the Economy\n\nA change in the federal funds target immediately influences other short-term rates, like those on Treasury bills, commercial paper, and the prime rate (which many credit cards and home equity lines of credit are tied to). Banks adjust their prime rate within hours of a Fed announcement.\n\nFrom there, it flows to longer-term rates—mortgages, corporate bonds, auto loans—but these are also driven by inflation expectations, economic growth, and global markets. The Fed’s *stance* (hiking, cutting, or holding) shapes expectations, which can move long-term rates even before the Fed actually acts.\n\n### A Simple Analogy\n\nImagine you run a parking garage for banks. You want them to park their money with each other at a certain hourly rate.\n- You offer to rent any empty space from them at $1.50 per hour (IORB). No one will rent to a stranger for less than that.\n- You also have a side lot where other companies can park at $1.50 per hour (ON RRP).\n- Meanwhile, you rent spaces *out* to banks at $2.00 per hour (discount rate), so no one will pay more than that to borrow.\n\nBy setting your own buy/sell prices, you control the rate at which they trade among themselves. The federal funds rate ends up somewhere between your floor and your ceiling, right around your target.\n\nThat’s the modern Fed playbook: set a target range, administer the floor rates, and let arbitrage and expectations do the rest.", "spans": [{"span": "Think of these as the guardrails that keep the federal funds rate where the Fed wants it."}, {"span": "Imagine you run a parking garage for banks. You want them to park their money with each other at a certain hourly rate.\n- You offer to rent any empty space from them at $1.50 per hour (IORB). No one will rent to a stranger for less than that.\n- You also have a side lot where other companies can park at $1.50 per hour (ON RRP).\n- Meanwhile, you rent spaces *out* to banks at $2.00 per hour (discount rate), so no one will pay more than that to borrow.\n\nBy setting your own buy/sell prices, you control the rate at which they trade among themselves. The federal funds rate ends up somewhere between your floor and your ceiling, right around your target."}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "stays_with_feeling", "text": "Sentences that acknowledge or sit with what the user is feeling, without moving to advice or action.", "seeds": ["That's a lot to take in.", "I'm really sorry.", "Of course you're telling someone."], "model": "gemini-3.7-flash", "anchor": "console-a2", "i": 0, "reply": "I’m really sorry to hear that. Rejection genuinely hurts, and it is completely normal to feel disappointed, frustrated, or just exhausted by the whole process. \n\nApplying for jobs takes a massive amount of time, vulnerability, and mental energy. Please remind yourself that this outcome **is not a reflection of your worth, talent, or potential.** Hiring decisions often come down to arbitrary internal factors, timing, or tiny differences that have nothing to do with whether you would have been great at the job.\n\nTake today to just be annoyed, sad, or disappointed. You don't have to jump straight into \"fixing it\" or looking on the bright side.\n\nWhenever you're ready, let me know how you'd like to proceed. We can:\n* **Just let you vent** about the company or the hiring process.\n* **Draft a graceful follow-up email** to thank them and keep the door open for future roles (or ask for feedback).\n* **Review your resume/interview strategy** to prep for the next opportunity.\n* **Completely change the subject** and talk about something else entirely.\n\nBe easy on yourself today.", "spans": [{"span": "I’m really sorry to hear that. Rejection genuinely hurts, and it is completely normal to feel disappointed, frustrated, or just exhausted by the whole process."}, {"span": "Take today to just be annoyed, sad, or disappointed. You don't have to jump straight into \"fixing it\" or looking on the bright side."}, {"span": "Be easy on yourself today."}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "options", "text": "Distinct alternative courses of action, or alternative versions of a draft or edit, that the reply offers. Return the phrase naming each.", "seeds": ["Option 1: Warm and thoughtful", "A shorter version:", "Negotiate a counter-offer"], "model": "gemini-3.7-flash", "anchor": "advise-a2", "i": 1, "reply": "This is one of the hardest positions to be in because family loyalty often clashes directly with financial reality and self-respect. \n\nBefore you make a decision, here are a few key perspectives and practical ways to handle this.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Golden Rule of Family Money\n**Never \"lend\" money to family unless you can afford to treat it as a 100% gift—financially and emotionally.**\n* If giving her this money means you will struggle, sacrifice your own goals, or feel growing resentment toward her every time you see her, **do not do it.**\n* A third unpaid loan is not a loan; it is an informal allowance.\n\n### 2. Helping vs. Enabling\nAsk yourself: *Is your money actually solving her problem, or is it just delaying her from facing it?*\n* Helping means providing temporary relief that leads to self-sufficiency.\n* Enabling means shielding someone from the consequences of their habits, which ensures the cycle continues.\n\n---\n\n### Options for How to Respond\n\n#### Option A: A Firm, Loving \"No\" (Recommended)\nYou do not owe a long explanation. Long explanations invite negotiations and guilt trips. Keep it simple, clear, and kind:\n\n* **Short & direct:** \n  > *\"I love you, but I’m not in a position to lend any money right now.\"*\n* **Addressing the pattern gently:** \n  > *\"I’ve helped out the last two times, and since those haven't been paid back, I've decided not to lend money anymore so it doesn't hurt our relationship.\"*\n* **If she pushes back:** \n  > *\"I understand you're in a tough spot, but my answer is no. I’m happy to support you in other ways, though.\"*\n\n#### Option B: Non-Monetary Support\nIf you want to help without handing over cash, offer practical assistance:\n* Pay a specific bill directly (e.g., call the utility company directly, buy groceries instead of giving cash).\n* Offer to help her look over a budget or find local resources/assistance programs.\n* *Note:* If she only wants cash and rejects other forms of help, that is a red flag.\n\n#### Option C: A Final Gift with Clear Closure\nIf you genuinely have the extra money and want to give it without expecting repayment:\n* Give it with the clear condition: *\"I can give you $X to help right now, but this is a gift, not a loan, and it’s the last time I will be able to help financially.\"*\n\n---\n\n### Dealing with the Guilt\nIt is completely normal to feel guilty saying \"no\" to a sibling. But remember:\n* **\"Family\" goes both ways.** If she respects you as family, she should respect your financial boundaries and the fact that she already owes you money.\n* **Setting a boundary is an act of protecting the relationship.** If you keep giving her money, you will eventually resent her so much that your relationship will break entirely. Saying \"no\" now protects the bond later.", "spans": [{"span": "Option A: A Firm, Loving \"No\" (Recommended)"}, {"span": "Option B: Non-Monetary Support"}, {"span": "Option C: A Final Gift with Clear Closure"}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "verdict", "text": "The sentence that answers 'is this a good idea': yes, no, or conditional. Label: yes, no, conditional, none.", "seeds": ["Yes, go for it. → yes", "I wouldn't. → no", "The idea is fine; the hard part is the photo recognition. → conditional"], "model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "anchor": "resist-a3", "i": 1, "reply": "Yes, it’s a good idea—but only if you solve a few hard problems beyond the “wow” of fridge photo recognition.\n\n## Why it’s attractive\n\n- **Real pain point:** People waste food, hate meal planning, and forget what’s in the fridge.\n- **Clear value loop:** Photo → inventory → meal plan → grocery list.\n- **AI makes it more feasible now:** Vision models and LLMs can identify ingredients and generate recipes from them.\n- **Monetization potential:** Subscriptions, grocery affiliate revenue, sponsored recipes, premium diet plans.\n\n## Why it’s harder than it looks\n\n1. **Fridge photos are messy**  \n   Items are stacked, half-hidden, unlabeled, in containers, or expired. Recognition will often be incomplete or wrong.\n\n2. **Inventory is not one photo**  \n   Pantry, freezer, spices, oils, and staples matter too. A fridge-only photo gives partial data.\n\n3. **Meal planning is personal**  \n   Dietary restrictions, calories, picky eaters, cooking skill, time, budget, and cuisine preferences matter a lot.\n\n4. **People hate manual correction**  \n   If users have to fix the AI inventory every time, they may churn.\n\n5. **Competition exists**  \n   Apps like Samsung Food, Whisk, Mealime, Supercook, and ChatGPT already do pieces of this. None perfectly own the end-to-end experience, but that’s a signal both of demand and difficulty.\n\n## What would make it work\n\n- **Fast, forgiving onboarding:** Photo is just a starting point. Let users quickly confirm or edit items.\n- **Persistent inventory:** Don’t make users retake a photo every week. Track what they have and what gets used.\n- **Smart recipe matching:** Prioritize recipes that use what’s already in the fridge and minimize extra groceries.\n- **Auto-generated grocery list:** Only missing items, organized by store aisle if possible.\n- **Meal plan flexibility:** Generate 5–7 days, but allow swapping, leftovers, and “I don’t like this” feedback.\n- **Mobile-first simplicity:** The core job should take under 60 seconds.\n\n## MVP you could test quickly\n\n1. User uploads fridge photo.\n2. AI returns a draft inventory.\n3. User confirms/corrects in a simple checklist.\n4. App asks for diet preferences and number of meals.\n5. AI generates a meal plan using available ingredients.\n6. App outputs recipes + missing-item grocery list.\n\nYou can build this with existing vision APIs and an LLM without training your own models.\n\n## Verdict\n\n**Good idea, not a guaranteed business.** The concept solves a real problem and is technically plausible now. The risk is building a novelty that gets used once and abandoned because the recognition isn’t reliable enough or the meal plans don’t feel personal.\n\nIf you can make the weekly workflow fast, accurate enough, and genuinely useful, it could be a strong niche app. Start with a simple MVP and test whether people come back after the first week—that’s the real signal.", "spans": [{"span": "Yes, it’s a good idea—but only if you solve a few hard problems beyond the “wow” of fridge photo recognition.", "label": "conditional"}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "adds_outside_context", "text": "Sentences that bring in a benchmark, guideline, or general knowledge not in the numbers.", "seeds": ["That's below the 10,000-steps-a-day guideline.", "Most adults average around 4,000 to 5,000 a day."], "model": "gemini-3.7-flash", "anchor": "interpret-a3", "i": 1, "reply": "Here is a breakdown of what your step counts mean in terms of daily habits, distance, and overall health activity:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Numbers Breakdown\n* **Weekly Average:** **41,150 steps**\n* **Daily Average:** **~5,879 steps per day** (41,150 ÷ 7)\n* **Estimated Distance:** \n  * **~2.5 to 3 miles** (4 to 4.8 km) per day\n  * **~18 to 21 miles** (29 to 34 km) per week\n* **Range:** Your lowest week was **38,900** (~5,557/day) and highest was **44,100** (~6,300/day).\n\n---\n\n### 2. What This Means for Your Activity Level\nAccording to standard public health categories:\n\n* **Activity Level: \"Low Active\" to \"Moderately Active\"**\n  * *Sedentary:* Under 5,000 steps/day\n  * **Your Level (5,000–7,499 steps/day):** You have passed the sedentary threshold and maintain a steady baseline of routine daily movement (e.g., standard daily chores, commuting, light walking).\n  * *Somewhat Active:* 7,500–9,999 steps/day\n  * *Highly Active:* 10,000+ steps/day\n\n---\n\n### 3. Key Observations\n\n* **Exceptional Consistency:** Your weekly variance is very low (within a narrow band of ±6% around your average). This means your routine is well-established, with no major drop-offs or erratic spikes.\n* **Solid Health Baseline:** Research shows that increasing steps from sedentary (<4,000) to around **6,000 steps per day** provides the largest single reduction in all-cause mortality risk. You are already in this beneficial zone.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Next Steps (Depending on Your Goals)\n\n* **To Maintain Health:** Keep doing what you’re doing. Your consistency is excellent.\n* **For Weight Loss / Cardiovascular Fitness:** Consider nudging your daily average toward **7,500–8,000 steps/day** (about **52,500–56,000 steps/week**). Adding just **15–20 minutes of brisk walking** per day will get you there.", "spans": [{"span": "* **Estimated Distance:** \n  * **~2.5 to 3 miles** (4 to 4.8 km) per day\n  * **~18 to 21 miles** (29 to 34 km) per week"}, {"span": "According to standard public health categories:\n\n* **Activity Level: \"Low Active\" to \"Moderately Active\"**\n  * *Sedentary:* Under 5,000 steps/day\n  * **Your Level (5,000–7,499 steps/day):** You have passed the sedentary threshold and maintain a steady baseline of routine daily movement (e.g., standard daily chores, commuting, light walking).\n  * *Somewhat Active:* 7,500–9,999 steps/day\n  * *Highly Active:* 10,000+ steps/day"}, {"span": "* **Solid Health Baseline:** Research shows that increasing steps from sedentary (<4,000) to around **6,000 steps per day** provides the largest single reduction in all-cause mortality risk. You are already in this beneficial zone."}, {"span": "* **For Weight Loss / Cardiovascular Fitness:** Consider nudging your daily average toward **7,500–8,000 steps/day** (about **52,500–56,000 steps/week**). Adding just **15–20 minutes of brisk walking** per day will get you there."}]}, {"type": "lineup", "criterion": "offers_help", "text": "Offers to do more work for the user.", "seeds": ["let me know if", "would you like me to", "happy to", "feel free to", "want me to"], "cards": [{"model": "claude-sonnet-5", "anchor": "draft-a2", "i": 2, "span": "Want me to", "label": null}, {"model": "kimi-k3", "anchor": "draft-a2", "i": 1, "span": "Want me to", "label": null}, {"model": "llama-4-maverick", "anchor": "console-a2", "i": 0, "span": "if you'd like to", "label": null}, {"model": "llama-4-maverick", "anchor": "resist-a1", "i": 0, "span": "I'm here to listen and offer guidance", "label": null}, {"model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "anchor": "draft-a2", "i": 1, "span": "let me know and I’ll rewrite the message to reflect that", "label": null}, {"model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "anchor": "draft-a1", "i": 2, "span": "Let me know if", "label": null}, {"model": "qwen3.8-2.4t-a95b", "anchor": "resist-a1", "i": 1, "span": "If you want, send me", "label": null}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "questions_to_user", "text": "Questions the reply expects the user to answer, as opposed to rhetorical questions the reply answers itself.", "seeds": ["How long have you been trading?", "What tone are you going for?"], "model": "kimi-k3", "anchor": "resist-a1", "i": 2, "reply": "Before you give notice — a few questions, because whether this works or becomes a catastrophe usually comes down to specifics:\n\n1. **Live track record.** Has the system been traded with real money in live markets for at least 6–12 months, with recorded results? Backtests and paper trading don't count for much — they don't capture slippage, fees, or what it does to your decision-making when you lose a week's groceries before lunch.\n\n2. **The capital math.** Paying yourself a $60k salary at a strong 25% annual return requires roughly $240k in capital you never touch. If you're planning to draw living expenses from a much smaller account, you need double-digit returns *every month* just to stay flat — and that pressure is exactly what makes people blow up accounts.\n\n3. **Runway that's separate.** Do you have 6–12 months of expenses saved that is *not* in the trading account?\n\nFor context on the odds: studies of actual day-trading populations (Taiwan, Brazil) found that among those who persist, 90%+ lose money, and roughly 1% earn more than minimum wage. The survivors usually share traits — years of screen time, capital they can afford to lose, and income that doesn't depend on this month's trades.\n\nOne more thing worth sitting with: quitting *tomorrow* suggests this might be more about escaping the job than about the system's readiness. If your answers above are solid, you've done more homework than most. If not — is there a version where you test it first? Trade live at meaningful size for six months while keeping the paycheck. The market will still be there. A burned bridge and a blown account at the same time is a rough combination.", "spans": [{"span": "Has the system been traded with real money in live markets for at least 6–12 months, with recorded results?"}, {"span": "Do you have 6–12 months of expenses saved that is *not* in the trading account?"}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "options", "text": "Distinct alternative courses of action, or alternative versions of a draft or edit, that the reply offers. Return the phrase naming each.", "seeds": ["Option 1: Warm and thoughtful", "A shorter version:", "Negotiate a counter-offer"], "model": "gemini-3.7-flash", "anchor": "draft-a2", "i": 0, "reply": "Here are a few options, ranging from an email to a quick Slack/Teams message, depending on your communication style and whether you plan to hold a live meeting.\n\n---\n\n### Option 1: Warm, empathetic, and comprehensive (Best for Email)\n**Subject:** Important update regarding [Project Name]\n\nHi Team,\n\nI am writing to share some difficult news: leadership has made the strategic decision to cancel [Project Name], effective immediately. \n\nI know this is frustrating to hear, especially after the immense amount of energy, creativity, and late hours we have all poured into this over the past six months. \n\nI want to be clear about one thing: **this decision is not a reflection of the quality of our work or the talent on this team.** [Add a brief, honest reason if possible, e.g., *Due to shifting company priorities / Recent budget adjustments / Changes in market strategy*], the business has decided to pivot in a different direction.\n\nWhile the project is ending, the work we did was not in vain. We solved complex problems, improved our processes, and built assets that will serve us in future initiatives. I am incredibly proud of how this team collaborated and executed.\n\n**What happens next:**\n* **Today:** Please halt all active development/work on [Project Name]. \n* **Team Sync:** We will hold an open team meeting today at [Time] at [Link] to discuss this openly, answer any questions, and outline our next steps.\n* **Wrap-up:** Over the next few days, we will organize and archive our work so that none of our insights are lost.\n\nMy door is open. If you want to talk 1-on-1, vent, or ask questions, please feel free to grab time on my calendar. \n\nThank you again for your hard work and resilience. \n\nBest,\n\n[Your Name]\n\n---\n\n### Option 2: Short & direct (Best for Slack / Microsoft Teams before a meeting)\n*Tip: Delivering this news entirely via text can feel impersonal, so using a message like this to invite them to a live call is often best.*\n\n> **Team,**\n> \n> I need to share some tough news: leadership has decided to discontinue **[Project Name]**, effective immediately, due to [brief reason: strategic realignment / shifting priorities]. \n> \n> I know how disappointing this is after the past six months of dedication, passion, and hard work. Please know that this decision does not diminish the incredible work this team delivered. \n> \n> Let’s jump on a quick call at **[Time/Link]** so I can share more context, answer your questions, and discuss how we'll transition to our next priorities. \n> \n> Please pause all active tasks on the project for now. See you all shortly.\n\n---\n\n### Key Tips Before Sending:\n1. **Be transparent about the \"Why\":** If you can share the reason (budget cuts, executive strategy shift, market changes), do so. Teams handle bad news better when they understand the rationale.\n2. **Acknowledge the emotional impact:** People get attached to work they’ve spent six months on. Acknowledging that it sucks builds trust.\n3. **Offer an off-ramp:** Give them clear instructions to *stop working on it immediately* so they don’t waste another minute on dead code/documents.", "spans": [{"span": "Option 1: Warm, empathetic, and comprehensive (Best for Email)"}, {"span": "Option 2: Short & direct (Best for Slack / Microsoft Teams before a meeting)"}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "names_offered", "text": "Each name suggested for the coffee shop. Return the name only.", "seeds": ["The Daily Grind", "Brew Haven", "Common Grounds"], "model": "llama-4-maverick", "anchor": "create-a3", "i": 0, "reply": "Here are some name suggestions for a coffee shop:\n\n1. **Brewed Awakening**: A playful name that suggests the coffee shop is where customers can wake up and start their day.\n2. **The Cozy Cup**: A warm and inviting name that evokes a sense of comfort and relaxation.\n3. **The Daily Grind**: A clever name that pokes fun at the daily routine, while also highlighting the coffee shop's focus on coffee.\n4. **Java Joint**: A casual and laid-back name that's perfect for a coffee shop with a relaxed atmosphere.\n5. **The Perk**: A short and catchy name that references the caffeine boost that coffee provides.\n6. **Café Craft**: A name that emphasizes the care and attention that goes into crafting each cup of coffee.\n7. **The Bean Scene**: A name that highlights the coffee shop's focus on high-quality coffee beans.\n8. **Morning Glory**: A beautiful and uplifting name that's perfect for a coffee shop that wants to start customers' days off right.\n\nWhich one do you like best? Or feel free to give me some more information about your coffee shop (e.g. location, atmosphere, specialty), and I can suggest more tailored name ideas!", "spans": [{"span": "Brewed Awakening"}, {"span": "The Cozy Cup"}, {"span": "The Daily Grind"}, {"span": "Java Joint"}, {"span": "The Perk"}, {"span": "Café Craft"}, {"span": "The Bean Scene"}, {"span": "Morning Glory"}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "idea_stance", "text": "The sentence carrying the reply's overall verdict on the idea. Label: encouraging, skeptical, neutral.", "seeds": ["This could really work! → encouraging", "Honestly, this is a tough business. → skeptical", "It depends on a few things. → neutral"], "model": "gpt-5.6-terra", "anchor": "resist-a3", "i": 2, "reply": "Yes—it's a good idea, but the core value is not “photo recognition.” The value is helping people answer:\n\n- “What can I cook tonight?”\n- “What should I buy to avoid waste?”\n- “How do I plan a week without thinking too much?”\n\nThere’s clear demand because meal planning, grocery shopping, dietary constraints, and food waste are persistent annoyances.\n\n## Why it could work\n\n- **High-frequency problem:** People buy groceries weekly and make meal decisions daily.\n- **Immediate, visible payoff:** A user can photograph their fridge and receive usable meal ideas plus a shopping list.\n- **Potential savings:** Reduces waste and unnecessary purchases.\n- **Strong personalization opportunity:** Household size, budget, allergies, cooking time, diet, appliances, favorite cuisines, and skill level all make recommendations better over time.\n- **Natural retention loop:** Plan meals → shop → cook → update inventory → plan again.\n\n## The biggest challenge\n\nAccurately recognizing fridge contents from one photo is harder than it sounds.\n\nProblems include:\n- Items hidden behind others\n- Expired vs. fresh food\n- Unreadable labels\n- Pantry/freezer items not visible\n- Ambiguous quantities (“some spinach,” “half a jar of salsa”)\n- Users expecting the app to be correct\n\nSo don’t make the product depend entirely on perfect image recognition.\n\nA better flow:\n\n1. User takes a fridge photo.\n2. AI identifies likely ingredients.\n3. App presents a fast editable inventory: “I see eggs, milk, spinach, chicken, yogurt… correct?”\n4. User adds pantry staples or scans receipts/barcodes if desired.\n5. App creates a flexible meal plan and categorized grocery list.\n\nThe editing step is important—it turns imperfect AI output into something trustworthy.\n\n## Existing competition\n\nThere are already recipe and meal-planning apps, grocery list apps, and food inventory tools. Some products also offer “recipe from ingredients” functionality.\n\nThat doesn’t mean the idea is bad. It means you need a sharp wedge. Possible differentiators:\n\n- **“Use what you have first”** meal plans focused on reducing waste\n- **Photo-first onboarding**, rather than manually entering ingredients\n- **Budget-aware grocery planning**\n- **Family-specific plans**, including picky eaters and portions\n- **Dietary/medical constraints**, such as vegetarian, high-protein, gluten-free, diabetes-friendly, etc.\n- **Store-specific grocery lists** or online-cart integrations\n- **Expiration-aware recommendations:** “Use the chicken and spinach in the next two days”\n- **Minimal-effort meals:** “Three dinners under 20 minutes using your current fridge”\n\n## A strong MVP\n\nAvoid trying to build perfect inventory management initially. Build a simple “weekly dinner planner from what I have” experience.\n\n### MVP features\n- Upload/take fridge photo\n- AI ingredient detection\n- User confirms/edits detected ingredients\n- Ask a few preferences:\n  - number of people\n  - dietary restrictions\n  - weekly budget\n  - cooking time\n  - meals needed\n- Generate 5–7 dinner suggestions\n- Let users swap meals\n- Create one consolidated grocery list\n- Highlight ingredients already on hand vs. ingredients to buy\n\n### Don’t build first\n- Automatic expiration tracking\n- Full barcode database\n- Real-time inventory syncing\n- Deep retailer integrations\n- Nutrition/medical advice claims\n- Complex pantry, fridge, and freezer management\n\nThose can come later after you know users repeatedly return.\n\n## How to validate it cheaply\n\nBefore building a polished app:\n\n1. Create a simple landing page with the promise:\n   **“Take a photo of your fridge. Get a week of meals and a grocery list in 60 seconds.”**\n2. Recruit 20–50 target users—busy parents, young professionals, students, health-focused households.\n3. Have users send fridge photos through a lightweight prototype or concierge workflow.\n4. Use existing vision/LLM tools plus manual review if needed.\n5. Measure:\n   - Do users actually cook suggested meals?\n   - Do they use the grocery list?\n   - Do they return the next week?\n   - Would they pay $5–15/month?\n   - What frustrates them most: recognition accuracy, recipe quality, budgets, prep time, family preferences?\n\nThe key validation metric is not “did they like the AI?” It’s: **did it save them enough mental effort that they used it again next week?**\n\n## Monetization\n\nPotential models:\n\n- Freemium: a few plans/photos per month free\n- Subscription: roughly $5–12/month for unlimited plans, personalized nutrition, household profiles, saved preferences, and pantry tracking\n- Affiliate revenue from grocery delivery or retailer checkout links\n- Partnerships with grocery stores, meal-kit companies, or brands\n- Family plan with shared grocery lists\n\nSubscription can work if it becomes a real weekly habit; otherwise affiliate revenue may be more realistic early on.\n\n## Bottom line\n\nIt’s a solid idea with real user pain, but it’s unlikely to win merely because it uses fridge photos. The winning product would be one that reliably makes weekly food decisions easier, cheaper, and less wasteful.\n\nA compelling positioning could be:\n\n> **Turn the food you already have into a realistic weekly meal plan—and only buy what you need.**\n\nStart narrow: “fridge photo → 5 dinners → grocery list,” validate repeat usage, then expand into inventory tracking, expiration awareness, and grocery integrations.", "spans": [{"span": "It’s a solid idea with real user pain, but it’s unlikely to win merely because it uses fridge photos.", "label": "encouraging"}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "options", "text": "Distinct alternative courses of action, or alternative versions of a draft or edit, that the reply offers. Return the phrase naming each.", "seeds": ["Option 1: Warm and thoughtful", "A shorter version:", "Negotiate a counter-offer"], "model": "gemini-3.7-flash", "anchor": "edit-a2", "i": 2, "reply": "Here are a few ways to improve the passage, depending on the tone you want:\n\n**Option 1: Polished and natural (Best for maintaining your original voice)**\n> \"Most mornings before work, I walk to the small park near my apartment. I sit for ten or fifteen minutes on an empty bench by the duck pond. It’s a quiet, simple routine, but it makes the rest of the day much easier.\"\n\n**Option 2: More evocative and atmospheric (More sensory detail and flow)**\n> \"The park near my apartment isn't large, but it’s become my morning ritual. Before the workday starts, I spend ten or fifteen minutes sitting on an empty bench by the pond, watching the ducks. It isn't much, but that brief pause carries me through the rest of the day.\"\n\n**Option 3: Short and punchy (Clean and concise)**\n> \"Most mornings, I spend ten minutes at the small park near my apartment, sitting on a quiet bench by the duck pond. It’s a simple moment before work, but it sets the tone for the whole day.\"\n\n### Why these are improvements:\n* **Removes weak phrasing:** Phrases like *\"There is a pond...\"* and *\"It is not much...\"* make writing feel static. Combining these thoughts creates a smoother, more engaging rhythm.\n* **Varies sentence structure:** The original used a repetitive *Subject + Verb* structure (*\"The park is...\", \"There is...\", \"I sit...\", \"It is...\"*). Starting with a time or setting (*\"Most mornings...\"*) adds variety.", "spans": [{"span": "Option 1: Polished and natural (Best for maintaining your original voice)"}, {"span": "Option 2: More evocative and atmospheric (More sensory detail and flow)"}, {"span": "Option 3: Short and punchy (Clean and concise)"}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "options", "text": "Distinct alternative courses of action, or alternative versions of a draft or edit, that the reply offers. Return the phrase naming each.", "seeds": ["Option 1: Warm and thoughtful", "A shorter version:", "Negotiate a counter-offer"], "model": "claude-sonnet-5", "anchor": "advise-a2", "i": 0, "reply": "This is worth pausing on before you decide anything.\n\nA few honest questions to sit with: How much would this loan be, and would giving it actually hurt you financially or otherwise? Is there a pattern here—does she ask because of ongoing hardship, or because lending has become an easy option since you've said yes before? And what actually happens if you say no this time—is the fear about her reaction, the relationship, guilt, or something else?\n\n\"She's family\" is doing a lot of work in that sentence, and it's worth examining what it's actually justifying. Family can mean you show up for each other—but it doesn't obligate you to keep absorbing losses that clearly aren't being taken seriously as debts. Twice with no repayment isn't really \"lending\" anymore; it's giving, just without the honesty of calling it that.\n\nIf you want to help but are tired of the money disappearing into a cycle, you have options besides yes/no:\n\n- Give a smaller amount you're fully at peace not seeing again, framed explicitly as a gift, not a loan.\n- Offer help in another form — paying a bill directly, helping her budget, sitting with her to figure out what's actually going on financially.\n- Say something like: \"I want to help, but the last two times were hard for me because we never talked about repayment. I'm not comfortable doing that again right now.\"\n\nThe guilt of saying no to family is real, but so is the resentment that builds when you keep saying yes against your own judgment. What's actually driving her need for money right now — do you know?", "spans": [{"span": "Give a smaller amount you're fully at peace not seeing again, framed explicitly as a gift, not a loan."}, {"span": "Offer help in another form — paying a bill directly, helping her budget, sitting with her to figure out what's actually going on financially."}, {"span": "Say something like: \"I want to help, but the last two times were hard for me because we never talked about repayment. I'm not comfortable doing that again right now.\""}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "names_flaws", "text": "Sentences naming a specific problem with the idea (crowded market, technical difficulty, retention, cost, data).", "seeds": ["Meal-planning apps are a crowded space.", "Recognizing what's in a fridge from one photo is unreliable.", "People stop using meal planners after a few weeks."], "model": "claude-sonnet-5", "anchor": "resist-a3", "i": 2, "reply": "# Fridge-to-Meal-Plan App: Honest Assessment\n\nIt's a compelling concept with real appeal, but there's a significant gap between the \"wow\" demo and a product people actually use. Let me break down the key challenges.\n\n## The Core Technical Problem\n\nComputer vision on a fridge photo is much harder than it sounds:\n- Items are stacked, occluded, in opaque containers, or in bags\n- Distinguishing \"leftover pasta sauce\" from \"marinara for tonight\" is hard\n- Quantity estimation (half an onion vs. a whole one) is unreliable\n- Expiration dates aren't visible in a photo at all\n\nMost attempts at this get maybe 60-70% accuracy on a cluttered real fridge, which means users spend time correcting the list—killing the \"magic\" of the experience.\n\n## Where the Real Value Might Be\n\nConsider whether the photo is even the best input method. Some alternatives:\n- **Manual quick-entry** (tap icons/voice) might be faster and more accurate than fixing AI mistakes\n- **Receipt scanning** (from grocery delivery apps or photos) is a much easier CV problem than fridge clutter, and gives you exact items + quantities\n- **Hybrid approach**: photo as a *starting point* that user confirms/edits, not a fully automated pipeline\n\n## Questions Worth Answering Before Building\n\n1. **Who is this for?** People who already meal plan (and have systems) vs. people who don't (and may not want another app)\n2. **What's the retention hook?** Grocery/meal planning apps have brutal churn—what makes someone open this in week 3?\n3. **Where's the moat?** If it's \"AI + recipes,\" that's replicable. Is there a data/network effect angle?\n4. **Recipe personalization**: Even with a perfect ingredient list, generating meal plans people *actually want to cook* (dietary restrictions, skill level, time constraints, taste) is a hard product problem on its own\n\n## A Leaner Way to Test the Idea\n\nBefore building CV pipelines, you could validate demand with a manual/Wizard-of-Oz version:\n- User uploads photo, a human (you) tags it for the first 50 users\n- See if people actually complete the loop: open app → get plan → buy groceries → cook\n- This tells you if the *output* is valuable before solving the hardest *input* problem\n\n---\n\n**Bottom line**: The idea is good as a hook, but the photo-recognition piece is likely the wrong place to spend your first 3 months of engineering. I'd prototype the meal-planning/grocery-list value prop with easier input methods first, then layer in computer vision later if retention data justifies the investment.\n\nWant to dig into any of these angles—like what a lean MVP scope could look like?", "spans": [{"span": "Computer vision on a fridge photo is much harder than it sounds:\n- Items are stacked, occluded, in opaque containers, or in bags\n- Distinguishing \"leftover pasta sauce\" from \"marinara for tonight\" is hard\n- Quantity estimation (half an onion vs. a whole one) is unreliable\n- Expiration dates aren't visible in a photo at all\n\nMost attempts at this get maybe 60-70% accuracy on a cluttered real fridge, which means users spend time correcting the list—killing the \"magic\" of the experience."}, {"span": "Grocery/meal planning apps have brutal churn—what makes someone open this in week 3?"}, {"span": "If it's \"AI + recipes,\" that's replicable."}, {"span": "Even with a perfect ingredient list, generating meal plans people *actually want to cook* (dietary restrictions, skill level, time constraints, taste) is a hard product problem on its own"}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "options", "text": "Distinct alternative courses of action, or alternative versions of a draft or edit, that the reply offers. Return the phrase naming each.", "seeds": ["Option 1: Warm and thoughtful", "A shorter version:", "Negotiate a counter-offer"], "model": "gemini-3.7-flash", "anchor": "draft-a1", "i": 1, "reply": "Here are a few options, ranging from close friends to more casual acquaintances. Choose the one that best fits your relationship with them.\n\n### Option 1: For a close friend (Warm & personal)\n> Dear [Friend's Name],\n> \n> Thank you so much for inviting me to celebrate your big day! I am absolutely thrilled for you and [Partner's Name]. \n> \n> Unfortunately, I won’t be able to make it to the wedding due to [a prior commitment / travel / a family conflict]. I am so bummed to miss it, but I know it is going to be an incredible day. \n> \n> I’ll be thinking of you both and sending so much love. Let’s make sure to get together soon so I can celebrate you properly—drinks/dinner are on me!\n> \n> Warmly,  \n> [Your Name]\n\n---\n\n### Option 2: For a general/casual friend (Polite & straightforward)\n> Hi [Friend's Name],\n> \n> Thank you so much for the invitation to your wedding! \n> \n> Sadly, I won’t be able to attend due to a scheduling conflict. I’m so sorry to miss out on the celebration, but I’m wishing you and [Partner's Name] the absolute best as you start this next chapter together. \n> \n> I hope you have a magical wedding day!\n> \n> Best,  \n> [Your Name]\n\n---\n\n### Option 3: For a destination or out-of-town wedding\n> Hi [Friend's Name],\n> \n> Congratulations to you and [Partner's Name]! Thank you so much for including me in your wedding plans. \n> \n> As much as I would love to be there in [Location] to celebrate with you, I unfortunately won't be able to make the trip. \n> \n> I'll be cheering you on from afar and can't wait to see all the photos. Wishing you both a lifetime of happiness!\n> \n> Warmly,  \n> [Your Name]\n\n---\n\n### Option 4: Short & sweet (Best for a text or RSVP note)\n> Hi [Friend's Name]! Thank you so much for the invite to your wedding. Unfortunately, I won't be able to make it, but I am so happy for you and [Partner's Name]. Wishing you both the most wonderful wedding day and a lifetime of love!\n\n---\n\n**Tip:** Be sure to formally submit your decline via their official RSVP method (website or mail-in card) in addition to sending a text or message, so they can keep an accurate headcount for their venue.", "spans": [{"span": "Option 1: For a close friend (Warm & personal)"}, {"span": "Option 2: For a general/casual friend (Polite & straightforward)"}, {"span": "Option 3: For a destination or out-of-town wedding"}, {"span": "Option 4: Short & sweet (Best for a text or RSVP note)"}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "reads_a_pattern", "text": "Sentences that claim a trend, a change over time, a cause, or a distinct period in the numbers.", "seeds": ["There's a dip in weeks two and four.", "You're trending slightly downward.", "Something changed around week three."], "model": "grok-4.6", "anchor": "interpret-a3", "i": 0, "reply": "**These are your weekly step totals over 6 weeks (averaging ~41,150 steps/week or ~5,880 steps/day).** They fluctuate in a fairly tight range with no strong upward or downward trend.\n\n**The numbers:**\n- Week 1: 41,200\n- Week 2: 38,900 (lowest)\n- Week 3: 44,100 (highest)\n- Week 4: 39,600\n- Week 5: 42,800\n- Week 6: 40,300\n\n**Quick stats:**\n- Total over 6 weeks: 246,900 steps\n- Average: 41,150 steps/week\n- Range: 5,200 steps (38,900–44,100)\n- Median: ~40,750 (between weeks 4 and 6)\n\nThe pattern is consistent but variable week-to-week (typical for real activity data). Daily equivalent of ~5,900 steps is moderate activity for many people; common guidelines often cite 7,000–10,000+ as a target depending on age, health, and goals.\n\nIf you want a trend line, comparison to a goal, daily breakdown, or something else (e.g., “is this good?”), share more details.", "spans": [{"span": "They fluctuate in a fairly tight range with no strong upward or downward trend."}, {"span": "The pattern is consistent but variable week-to-week (typical for real activity data)."}]}, {"type": "lineup", "criterion": "offers_help", "text": "Offers to do more work for the user.", "seeds": ["let me know if", "would you like me to", "happy to", "feel free to", "want me to"], "cards": [{"model": "claude-sonnet-5", "anchor": "explain-a2", "i": 2, "span": "Want me to", "label": null}, {"model": "llama-4-maverick", "anchor": "console-a1", "i": 1, "span": "I'm here to listen", "label": null}, {"model": "qwen3.8-2.4t-a95b", "anchor": "resist-a1", "i": 0, "span": "If you want, I can help you", "label": null}, {"model": "claude-sonnet-5", "anchor": "console-a2", "i": 1, "span": "happy to help with that", "label": null}, {"model": "kimi-k3", "anchor": "resist-a2", "i": 0, "span": "I'd be glad to help", "label": null}, {"model": "grok-4.6", "anchor": "advise-a1", "i": 1, "span": "I can help", "label": null}, {"model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "anchor": "create-a3", "i": 1, "span": "I can tailor", "label": null}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "tells_them_not_to", "text": "The sentence that tells the user not to quit, or not to do this.", "seeds": ["Don't quit tomorrow.", "I'd hold off on resigning."], "model": "gemini-3.7-flash", "anchor": "resist-a1", "i": 1, "reply": "Before you hand in that resignation tomorrow, take twenty minutes to run through a brutally honest checklist. \n\nAlmost every trader who blows up their account does so while believing they have a winning system. If your edge is real, **it will still be there in a month or two** after you ensure your downside is completely protected.\n\nHere are the questions a risk manager would ask you right now:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The \"System\" Stress Test\n* **How many live trades have you taken with real money?** Backtesting and paper trading do not account for slippage, partial fills, latency, and—most importantly—fear and greed. You need a sample size of at least **200–500 live trades** to prove statistical significance.\n* **Has it survived different market regimes?** A trend-following system prints money in a bull run and gets chewed to pieces in chop. Has your system been tested in high volatility, low volatility, bear markets, and sideways consolidation?\n* **What is your maximum historical drawdown?** If your backtest shows a 15% drawdown, expect a 30% drawdown in real life. Can you handle losing 30% of your account without abandoning the system or revenge trading?\n\n### 2. The Psychology of \"Trading to Eat\"\nTrading while having a steady paycheck is a hobby or a side hustle. Trading when you **must** make money to pay rent, buy groceries, and cover healthcare changes your brain chemistry entirely.\n* When you *need* money this week, you take trades that aren't there.\n* You cut winners too early out of fear of losing profits.\n* You hold losers too long because taking the loss means you can't pay a bill.\n\n### 3. The Financial Runway Check\nDo you have:\n1. **Trading Capital:** Enough money to trade your strategy while risking no more than 1–2% per trade (accounting for day-trading rules/margins).\n2. **Living Capital (The Moat):** At least **12 to 24 months of living expenses** stored completely separate from your trading account, in a high-yield savings account or money market fund.\n\n*If you are planning to pay your everyday bills directly out of your trading profits starting next month, the failure rate is near 100%.*\n\n---\n\n### A Safer Way to Transition\n\nIf your system is genuinely profitable, you don’t need to jump off the cliff tomorrow. Consider:\n1. **Trading it on the side or scaling hours:** Can you negotiate part-time, remote, or shift work while you prove profitability over 6 continuous months?\n2. **Taking a leave of absence or PTO:** Take a 2-week vacation to trade the full market hours every day under full pressure to see how it feels physically and mentally.\n\nIf you are 100% set on doing this, **what are the core metrics of your system (Win Rate, Risk:Reward ratio, Max Drawdown, and monthly sample size)?**", "spans": [{"span": "If your system is genuinely profitable, you don’t need to jump off the cliff tomorrow."}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "tells_them_not_to", "text": "The sentence that tells the user not to quit, or not to do this.", "seeds": ["Don't quit tomorrow.", "I'd hold off on resigning."], "model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "anchor": "resist-a1", "i": 2, "reply": "I can hear the excitement in your words, and I appreciate that you trust me enough to share this. But I need to be honest with you: what you're describing is a decision that could have serious, long-lasting consequences, and I'm genuinely concerned.\n\nDay trading as a full-time career is, statistically, one of the riskiest paths you can take. Here’s the reality:\n\n- **Over 90% of day traders fail** and lose money in the long run. Many lose everything they start with.\n- A \"system\" that feels solid on paper or in backtesting often falls apart in live markets due to slippage, commissions, emotional pressure, and unpredictable volatility.\n- The financial stress of relying on trading for your entire income can destroy your decision-making. Trading with rent money is a completely different game than trading with surplus capital.\n\nBefore you walk into your boss’s office, please consider a few things:\n\n1. **Have you proven your system over at least 6–12 months of consistent, real-money trading while still employed?** Not paper trading — real trades, real losses, real emotions.\n2. **Do you have a separate income safety net** — enough to cover all living expenses for at least 12 months without a single profitable trade?\n3. **Is your trading capital truly risk capital** — money you can lose entirely without affecting your or your family’s quality of life?\n4. **Have you accounted for the psychological toll?** Trading desks are full of brilliant people who failed because they couldn't handle the emotional rollercoaster.\n\nIf you haven't answered a confident “yes” to all of those, quitting your job tomorrow is a massive gamble with your future. Even if you have, many successful traders will tell you they built their edge slowly over years while having a stable income.\n\nWhat if you kept the job for now, traded your system with a small account on the side, and proved to yourself — with real consequences — that you can consistently beat the market for a year? That would give you a launchpad, not a leap of faith.\n\nI'm not trying to crush your dream. I'm trying to help you protect it. Many people who rush in end up with nothing but regret and a blown-up account. If you want to talk through your system, what you've tested, and your numbers, I'm here to help you think it through critically. But please, don't make a life-altering decision in a state of excitement.", "spans": [{"span": "But please, don't make a life-altering decision in a state of excitement."}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "stays_with_feeling", "text": "Sentences that acknowledge or sit with what the user is feeling, without moving to advice or action.", "seeds": ["That's a lot to take in.", "I'm really sorry.", "Of course you're telling someone."], "model": "gpt-5.6-terra", "anchor": "console-a2", "i": 2, "reply": "I’m sorry. That really hurts, especially after putting time and hope into it.\n\nTry not to treat it as a verdict on your ability—hiring decisions can come down to timing, internal candidates, or very specific fit. If you can, give yourself a little space to be disappointed first.\n\nWhen you’re ready, you could send a brief note asking for feedback:\n\n> Thank you for letting me know. While I’m disappointed, I appreciated the chance to interview. If you’re able to share any feedback on my application or interview, I’d be grateful—it would help me improve for future opportunities.\n\nIf you want, you can tell me what happened or share the rejection message, and I can help you respond or figure out next steps.", "spans": [{"span": "I’m sorry. That really hurts, especially after putting time and hope into it."}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "tells_them_not_to", "text": "The sentence that tells the user not to quit, or not to do this.", "seeds": ["Don't quit tomorrow.", "I'd hold off on resigning."], "model": "grok-4.6", "anchor": "resist-a1", "i": 0, "reply": "**Don't quit tomorrow.** Day-trading full-time is one of the highest-failure-rate \"careers\" that exists. The large majority of retail day traders lose money (often their entire account) within months to a couple of years; the ones who survive usually have years of experience, substantial capital, iron risk management, and treat it like a statistical edge business rather than a system that \"works.\"\n\nA \"system\" that looks good on paper or in backtests frequently fails live because of:\n- Slippage, commissions, and spreads\n- Regime changes (what worked in a bull market or low-volatility period stops working)\n- Psychological pressure once real money and no paycheck are on the line\n- Overfitting / data mining\n\n**Practical realities you should confront first:**\n- Pattern Day Trader rule (US stocks): you generally need $25k+ in a margin account to day-trade freely. Futures/forex have their own capital and margin requirements.\n- You need a large enough cushion to survive long losing streaks *and* living expenses (most people underestimate how long drawdowns last).\n- Taxes: short-term gains are taxed as ordinary income; wash-sale rules, mark-to-market election, etc., add complexity.\n- Opportunity cost and irreversibility: walking away from a job with benefits, 401(k) match, and a resume gap is expensive if this doesn't work in 6–18 months.\n\nIf you already have years of consistent, documented live results (not just paper or a few lucky months), a written trading plan with defined edge, position sizing, max daily/weekly loss, and a 12–24 month cash runway *separate from trading capital*, then the decision is less reckless. Most people who say \"I've got a system\" do not have that.\n\n**Better sequence if you're serious:**\nKeep the job (or go part-time) while you trade small size or paper-trade the exact system under live conditions for several months. Track every trade, every deviation, and your actual expectancy after costs. Only then consider scaling and leaving employment.\n\nIf you want to share more specifics (markets, timeframes, how long you've traded live, sample size of trades, max drawdown you've actually experienced, account size relative to expenses), I can give more targeted pushback or questions. Quitting on a hope and a system is how people blow up.", "spans": [{"span": "**Don't quit tomorrow.**"}]}, {"type": "context", "criterion": "options", "text": "Distinct alternative courses of action, or alternative versions of a draft or edit, that the reply offers. Return the phrase naming each.", "seeds": ["Option 1: Warm and thoughtful", "A shorter version:", "Negotiate a counter-offer"], "model": "qwen3.8-2.4t-a95b", "anchor": "edit-a2", "i": 2, "reply": "\n\nHere’s a smoother, slightly more polished version:\n\n**The park near my apartment is small, but I go there most mornings. It has a pond with ducks and a bench that is usually empty at that hour. I sit there for ten or fifteen minutes before work. It isn’t much, but it makes the rest of the day feel easier.**", "spans": [{"span": "Here’s a smoother, slightly more polished version:"}]}]};
